1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,961
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,961 --> 00:00:03,909
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,909 --> 00:00:05,370
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,600
PHILLIP ISOLA: So welcome.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,920
Happy Halloween.

6
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,480
Nice to see a few little costumes.

7
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:23,425
So today, we're going to talk about generative models.

8
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:25,800
Again, this will be our third in the sequence of lectures

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:27,880
on generative models.

10
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,280
And last lecture was a little bit math-heavy, a little bit

11
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,840
notation-heavy, a little bit of a slog.

12
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,440
This one, I hope, will be a bit more fun and lightweight.

13
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:40,840
We're going to get to see some applications of that math.

14
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,020
So here is the topics that we'll cover in this lecture.

15
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:48,520
We'll talk about the idea of structured prediction.

16
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,800
And I will go through a few different examples

17
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,760
in different domains.

18
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,700
All the domains will be images and text

19
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:56,325
because those are the two domains where

20
00:00:56,325 --> 00:00:58,640
this kind of technology is best developed.

21
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,250
But this technology will, in the future,

22
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:04,489
and is currently starting to be used in other domains, too.

23
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:07,710
But I'll talk about mapping images to images, text to text,

24
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:10,650
image to text, text to image.

25
00:01:10,650 --> 00:01:13,530
And then I'll end with a short section on,

26
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:15,990
can we do these mappings without having paired data,

27
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:19,970
without having supervision of the form of paired data--

28
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:21,150
in the form of paired data?

29
00:01:23,930 --> 00:01:29,430
So when I first encountered generative models,

30
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:31,130
it took me a little while to understand

31
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:35,010
why they are an important thing, especially

32
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:37,250
if you come across them in this generative AI

33
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,737
era when it was like--

34
00:01:39,737 --> 00:01:41,570
your first experience might have been you're

35
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:45,930
making up beautiful images or writing funny stories.

36
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:49,810
And that feels like it might be very useful for entertainment

37
00:01:49,810 --> 00:01:51,950
or there's some kind of frivolous art form.

38
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,825
But really, I think they're just fundamental.

39
00:01:53,825 --> 00:01:55,530
They're core to almost everything.

40
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:58,030
I think I've made that point in the previous lectures.

41
00:01:58,030 --> 00:02:01,210
But I think it will be most obvious in this lecture, where

42
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:04,850
I'll frame all problems that are predicting

43
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:09,410
high-dimensional structured data or information out of a network

44
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:11,570
as being appropriate to be treated

45
00:02:11,570 --> 00:02:13,170
with generative modeling.

46
00:02:13,170 --> 00:02:16,530
So we can call these data prediction problems--

47
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:18,662
in statistics and other areas, this is sometimes

48
00:02:18,662 --> 00:02:19,870
called structured prediction.

49
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:22,120
So the problem of outputting something that's not just

50
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:24,450
a single number or a single decision,

51
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,490
but is actually some high-dimensional structured

52
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:27,730
object--

53
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,410
this is the problem that generative models are well

54
00:02:30,410 --> 00:02:31,290
suited to.

55
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:33,750
And this is the problem, which is basically ubiquitous.

56
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:36,570
It's hard to imagine almost any problem that you'll come across

57
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:39,990
in your own work which would not be structured prediction.

58
00:02:39,990 --> 00:02:44,753
It's a weird historical artifact that we had this term,

59
00:02:44,753 --> 00:02:45,670
structured prediction.

60
00:02:45,670 --> 00:02:47,210
This is just everything.

61
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:49,585
But I'll define it on the next slide.

62
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:50,710
But here are some examples.

63
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:53,210
So imagine in and image out-- so this

64
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:55,500
is like the problem of detecting objects.

65
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,220
It's a high-dimensional structured object

66
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:59,140
which you're trying to predict, which

67
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:02,300
is an array of pixels that have a class assignment

68
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:04,940
for every input pixel.

69
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:07,720
Audio-- and it's structured data in, structured data out.

70
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:08,800
The output is a sentence.

71
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,760
It's not just a number or a single decision, a class.

72
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,660
It's a whole sentence.

73
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:19,180
This is speech recognition or automatic speech transcription.

74
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:23,400
Another one that's quite popular is describing an image in text,

75
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,420
or maybe a video in text, and outputting the entire image

76
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:27,420
or video--

77
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:29,620
so text-to-photo, which you may have

78
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:32,620
played with in things like Stable Diffusion or Midjourney

79
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:35,020
or Dall-E.

80
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:39,980
Another example is a input sequence of some kind of protein

81
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:42,440
sequence and output the 3D structure of that protein.

82
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,300
So again, you're not just trying to give one label to,

83
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,640
what is this protein?

84
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,640
You're trying to output a structured object.

85
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,700
The entire geometry is a high-dimensional object.

86
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:52,545
And it's an object that has structure.

87
00:03:52,545 --> 00:03:54,170
And what do I mean by having structure?

88
00:03:54,170 --> 00:03:55,587
This is going to be the definition

89
00:03:55,587 --> 00:03:56,990
of structured prediction.

90
00:03:56,990 --> 00:04:00,808
It means that I'm modeling a high-dimensional object X.

91
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:03,350
So my output that I'm trying to predict is a high-dimensional

92
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:07,150
object X. And it's an object which doesn't factorize

93
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:09,790
into independent--

94
00:04:09,790 --> 00:04:11,590
a product of these marginals.

95
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:13,550
So unstructured prediction was if I

96
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:16,430
want to model some high-dimensional data,

97
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:18,070
I will assume that all the dimensions

98
00:04:18,070 --> 00:04:19,550
are independent statistically.

99
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:24,030
So independent random variables means that the probabilities

100
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:25,250
factorize as a product.

101
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:29,455
So the joint distribution equals a product over each dimension.

102
00:04:29,455 --> 00:04:30,830
And if I don't have independence,

103
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:32,010
I don't have a product.

104
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:34,170
And then we call it structured prediction.

105
00:04:34,170 --> 00:04:36,990
And of course, you-- it's hard to imagine any setting where you

106
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:39,248
really have independence over your dimensions

107
00:04:39,248 --> 00:04:40,790
of your high-dimensional structured--

108
00:04:40,790 --> 00:04:43,165
of your high-dimensional object you're trying to predict,

109
00:04:43,165 --> 00:04:46,712
like text or, if it's a robot, the trajectory

110
00:04:46,712 --> 00:04:47,670
it has to move its arm.

111
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:49,545
And these are all high-dimensional structured

112
00:04:49,545 --> 00:04:50,550
objects.

113
00:04:50,550 --> 00:04:54,270
If I'm a self-driving car, how I navigate the city-- it's like,

114
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:56,457
every decision is not independent of all

115
00:04:56,457 --> 00:04:57,290
the other decisions.

116
00:04:57,290 --> 00:04:59,415
There's joint dependencies between those decisions.

117
00:04:59,415 --> 00:05:02,790
If I turned left, that affects what my next decision should be.

118
00:05:05,810 --> 00:05:08,270
So this is the setting that we're in.

119
00:05:08,270 --> 00:05:11,790
But let's first go back and see how people approached these data

120
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,170
prediction problems with unstructured models

121
00:05:14,170 --> 00:05:16,830
because even though it seems like a crazy idea now,

122
00:05:16,830 --> 00:05:17,730
it was simple.

123
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:19,510
And it's not terrible.

124
00:05:19,510 --> 00:05:21,170
There's reasons why that can work.

125
00:05:24,034 --> 00:05:28,430
So here's the unstructured way of modeling

126
00:05:28,430 --> 00:05:29,970
the colorization problem.

127
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:31,870
So I love this problem because it

128
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:35,528
captures all the key concepts in machine learning, in my opinion.

129
00:05:35,528 --> 00:05:37,070
I don't actually care that much about

130
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:38,887
colorization as a graphics problem.

131
00:05:38,887 --> 00:05:40,470
But I've often used this as an example

132
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:43,170
because it just has all the right properties.

133
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:46,170
So this is trying to predict the color of this shirt here.

134
00:05:46,170 --> 00:05:49,310
So what color do you think it is?

135
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:50,580
Who has the answer?

136
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:51,080
No one.

137
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:52,580
You don't know-- could be red.

138
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:53,740
It could be orange.

139
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,700
This is the grayscale version of the shirt.

140
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:57,000
So what color is it?

141
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,800
Well, maybe I know that this shirt is manufactured

142
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:02,720
by some company, and this shirt comes in two different colors.

143
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,920
I am going to imagine that the true probability of color

144
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:10,680
of this grayscale shirt conditioned

145
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:13,480
on the luminance value, the grayscale value--

146
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:15,800
this is going to be the data distribution that

147
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:18,340
is the way the actual universe works.

148
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:21,540
So this is the true distribution that describes this data.

149
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:24,240
I take shirts that are teal and shirts

150
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,440
that are pink with equal probability.

151
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:27,940
I convert them to a grayscale image.

152
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:31,320
And that's my observation.

153
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:35,120
So if I just try to predict the color using

154
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:39,480
the classical regression method, which is just

155
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:42,608
output a single number for-- a single continuous number,

156
00:06:42,608 --> 00:06:44,400
what color do I think that shirt is-- let's

157
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:46,840
imagine color is a one-dimensional space that

158
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:49,080
goes from teal to pink.

159
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:52,880
Well, with this point prediction method,

160
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:57,640
you can interpret that, if you want to, as a posterior

161
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,650
distribution over all the possible colors

162
00:06:59,650 --> 00:07:00,900
that this shirt could take on.

163
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,000
But you're outputting a delta function.

164
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,920
And it doesn't express anything about your uncertainty

165
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,300
over what the range of colors could be.

166
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,800
And it can't capture this multimodal distribution.

167
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:15,120
So in particular, if you are doing

168
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:18,360
least squares regression to output a scalar value, which

169
00:07:18,360 --> 00:07:20,920
is going to be your prediction of color,

170
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:26,023
the minimizer of the least squared objective over a data--

171
00:07:26,023 --> 00:07:27,440
a training distribution that looks

172
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:31,980
like this is going to be the average of the two colors.

173
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:36,640
So the average is the minimizer of the sum

174
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:41,400
of squared deviations from a--

175
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:43,080
the average of a set of data points

176
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:45,960
is a minimizer of the squared deviation from all the data

177
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:47,730
points.

178
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:52,210
So that's something to lock--

179
00:07:52,210 --> 00:07:53,350
slot away in your brain.

180
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:55,790
It's an important property that comes up a lot.

181
00:07:55,790 --> 00:08:00,430
The mean or the expected value of the data distribution,

182
00:08:00,430 --> 00:08:02,090
the mean of that data distribution,

183
00:08:02,090 --> 00:08:06,450
is going to be the minimizer of the least squares objective

184
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:07,850
regression problem.

185
00:08:07,850 --> 00:08:11,810
But notice the problem is that the mean

186
00:08:11,810 --> 00:08:14,510
has zero density under the true data distribution.

187
00:08:14,510 --> 00:08:18,370
So the mean is a terrible estimate of the color.

188
00:08:18,370 --> 00:08:22,090
Yes, if you predict the color, then your error

189
00:08:22,090 --> 00:08:24,850
will-- that's a single prediction that minimizes

190
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:26,750
the difference from the truth.

191
00:08:26,750 --> 00:08:29,730
Just say, well, if I'm here, I would sometimes get it wrong

192
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:30,810
because I would have--

193
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:33,070
it would have been teal, and I predicted pink.

194
00:08:33,070 --> 00:08:35,789
So I'll get half the time right, half the time wrong.

195
00:08:35,789 --> 00:08:39,370
But if I square the distance, then the wrong answers

196
00:08:39,370 --> 00:08:40,230
will dominate.

197
00:08:40,230 --> 00:08:43,730
So halfway in between is going to be the best

198
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:46,060
answer for minimizing my error.

199
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:48,620
But it's a terrible answer because that

200
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:51,160
has zero probability under the data distribution.

201
00:08:53,585 --> 00:08:55,460
So we could do a little bit better by saying,

202
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:57,680
no, let me try to model some uncertainty.

203
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:59,920
So I'm not going to have a true probabilistic model.

204
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:02,580
I'll try to have a max likelihood

205
00:09:02,580 --> 00:09:06,420
density fit to my data distribution, my training data.

206
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:08,700
And it will be a Gaussian in shape.

207
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:10,900
And that's great because now I am

208
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:13,900
modeling the variance, the uncertainty over what

209
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:14,887
the range of colors is.

210
00:09:14,887 --> 00:09:17,220
But this is-- this Gaussian is insufficient because it's

211
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:18,928
unimodal distribution.

212
00:09:18,928 --> 00:09:21,220
And the true data distribution that I'm trying to model

213
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:21,955
is bimodal.

214
00:09:21,955 --> 00:09:23,580
So you can't fit a bimodal distribution

215
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:27,060
with a unimodal distribution.

216
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:32,260
So one very common trick is to simply switch

217
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:33,760
to a categorical distribution.

218
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:36,580
So this is the hack that I mentioned

219
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:38,720
in the hacker's guide lecture-- that oftentimes,

220
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:41,580
if you just quantize your data, chop it up

221
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:44,260
into discrete classes, and then model

222
00:09:44,260 --> 00:09:48,300
a categorical distribution over the probability of each class,

223
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:49,720
it will work really well.

224
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:52,260
And here's one of the reasons why it works really well--

225
00:09:52,260 --> 00:09:55,480
because the categorical distribution,

226
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:59,140
which is just a set of numbers that sum to 1,

227
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:02,643
can express bimodal distributions.

228
00:10:02,643 --> 00:10:04,060
So the categorical distribution is

229
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:06,500
fully expressive over this quantized space.

230
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:09,553
You can represent any number of bumps.

231
00:10:09,553 --> 00:10:11,220
So the categorical distribution actually

232
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:14,180
completely worked for solving this problem up

233
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:15,240
to quantization error.

234
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:18,400
So the only issue is that I had to quantize my colors.

235
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,980
So now I don't have a continuous exact, precise color

236
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:21,660
I'm outputting.

237
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:24,540
I'm outputting that it's the teal class.

238
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:26,140
And there's some quantization error.

239
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:31,340
So the categorical distribution is pretty good.

240
00:10:31,340 --> 00:10:34,220
And that's one reason why we often use softmax regression,

241
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:37,060
because that models a categorical distribution

242
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:40,740
over a set of classes.

243
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:44,290
So that solved the problem of multimodality.

244
00:10:44,290 --> 00:10:46,790
So one problem with prediction is

245
00:10:46,790 --> 00:10:50,110
that there could be a multimodal distribution over outcomes,

246
00:10:50,110 --> 00:10:51,730
meaning more than one bump.

247
00:10:51,730 --> 00:10:54,310
And if that's the case, then regression-- least squares

248
00:10:54,310 --> 00:10:55,770
regression does not work.

249
00:10:55,770 --> 00:10:57,270
Point prediction does not work.

250
00:10:57,270 --> 00:11:00,015
But actually, classification does work.

251
00:11:00,015 --> 00:11:01,390
But there's another problem which

252
00:11:01,390 --> 00:11:04,070
classification suffers from.

253
00:11:04,070 --> 00:11:06,687
So this was only to predict one color.

254
00:11:06,687 --> 00:11:08,770
And if I really want to colorize that whole shirt,

255
00:11:08,770 --> 00:11:10,910
I need to predict the color of every single pixel

256
00:11:10,910 --> 00:11:12,310
in that shirt.

257
00:11:12,310 --> 00:11:15,430
So my output is actually a high-dimensional object.

258
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:19,130
It's not just one value in this colorization problem.

259
00:11:19,130 --> 00:11:22,950
It's going to be one value per pixel in the input image.

260
00:11:22,950 --> 00:11:25,670
So here's where classification fails.

261
00:11:25,670 --> 00:11:28,850
So classification, meaning outputting a--

262
00:11:28,850 --> 00:11:32,710
the-- my softmax distribution over the possible classes,

263
00:11:32,710 --> 00:11:36,750
will give me, in this case, equal probability

264
00:11:36,750 --> 00:11:38,960
of teal and pink.

265
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:42,120
And at this pixel, I'll say, it's

266
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:43,700
slightly more likely to be teal.

267
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,120
Maybe this-- the picture actually

268
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,880
has a little bit of shading and there's just 51% probability

269
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:50,653
of being teal at this location, for whatever reason,

270
00:11:50,653 --> 00:11:53,320
because there's a little shading right there that indicates it's

271
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:56,600
a little darker, in some sense.

272
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:59,180
Over here, maybe it's a bit brighter illumination.

273
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:02,940
But I now say it's-- well, it's slightly more likely to be pink.

274
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:05,480
There's some kind of dependency between the brightness

275
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,040
of the shirt and the color.

276
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:08,820
And that means it could be either color,

277
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:10,880
but it's slightly more likely to be pink.

278
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:12,580
And this will just always happen.

279
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:15,840
You'll always have your conditional distribution

280
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:17,880
over your predictions being spatially

281
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:21,360
varying to some degree if I actually have underlying--

282
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:24,683
the truth is almost equal probability, or in this case,

283
00:12:24,683 --> 00:12:26,600
I said the truth is exactly equal probability,

284
00:12:26,600 --> 00:12:28,102
of teal and pink.

285
00:12:28,102 --> 00:12:29,560
So my estimates of that probability

286
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:30,700
will be a little bit noisy.

287
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:32,580
And they'll vary from pixel to pixel.

288
00:12:32,580 --> 00:12:36,400
And what will happen then is I won't have consistency

289
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:38,320
between how I colorize the top of the shirt

290
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:41,160
and the bottom of the shirt because the probabilities

291
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:42,100
smoothly varied.

292
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:45,160
But they crossed this threshold where the argmax probability

293
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:47,000
changed.

294
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,760
So this means that I'm basically failing

295
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:51,160
to capture the joint dependency between this prediction

296
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:52,380
and that prediction.

297
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:54,980
The joint dependency says that they should be the same color.

298
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:57,600
But if I were to classify each pixel independently,

299
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,020
I would fail to capture that.

300
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:04,300
So generative models get around this.

301
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,560
The set of generative models we've talked about

302
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:09,120
get around this because they have two important properties.

303
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:13,173
One is-- well, this is almost like the definition--

304
00:13:13,173 --> 00:13:15,340
when we talk about generative AI, generative models,

305
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:15,980
this is what we mean.

306
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:17,600
These are the two properties we mean.

307
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:20,080
They can model a multimodal distribution.

308
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:23,320
So it's not just one Gaussian, but they can

309
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:25,640
model a complicated posterior.

310
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:27,980
And two is they model joint dependency.

311
00:13:27,980 --> 00:13:30,240
So it's a high-dimensional multimodal distribution

312
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:32,880
where the dimensions all have dependencies between them.

313
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:34,660
And if you have a model that can do this,

314
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:37,930
then we usually will think of that as a generative model.

315
00:13:37,930 --> 00:13:41,610
And the VAEs and GANs and diffusion models

316
00:13:41,610 --> 00:13:43,690
all can do this.

317
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:45,990
So that's the motivation.

318
00:13:45,990 --> 00:13:47,510
Any questions about that?

319
00:13:51,650 --> 00:13:54,050
So to me, it's just strange--

320
00:13:54,050 --> 00:13:55,530
it was only in the last few years

321
00:13:55,530 --> 00:13:57,370
that people really had models that

322
00:13:57,370 --> 00:14:00,530
can do both of these things in practice.

323
00:14:00,530 --> 00:14:03,170
In theory, we've thought about this for decades.

324
00:14:03,170 --> 00:14:05,470
But this is just so important.

325
00:14:05,470 --> 00:14:07,390
Every problem has this characteristic.

326
00:14:07,390 --> 00:14:08,970
So these generative models are just

327
00:14:08,970 --> 00:14:11,530
the thing that you really want to use for, I think,

328
00:14:11,530 --> 00:14:14,740
almost everything.

329
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:17,730
So now that you saw those two flaws

330
00:14:17,730 --> 00:14:21,090
with unstructured prediction, treating every pixel

331
00:14:21,090 --> 00:14:23,890
independently and outputting a continuous real number

332
00:14:23,890 --> 00:14:26,290
for the-- those pixels, let's look

333
00:14:26,290 --> 00:14:29,230
at some photos that might be a real photo,

334
00:14:29,230 --> 00:14:32,210
or it might be a colorized black and white photo that was--

335
00:14:32,210 --> 00:14:33,590
an AI system added color to.

336
00:14:33,590 --> 00:14:36,610
And so I want you to guess.

337
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:39,090
So raise your hand if you think that this is

338
00:14:39,090 --> 00:14:42,770
a real photo with real colors.

339
00:14:42,770 --> 00:14:43,450
A few people.

340
00:14:43,450 --> 00:14:44,825
Raise your hand if you think it's

341
00:14:44,825 --> 00:14:46,990
a fake photo with AI-generated colors.

342
00:14:46,990 --> 00:14:47,870
A lot of people.

343
00:14:47,870 --> 00:14:50,690
So somebody who said fake, tell me, what

344
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:55,690
artifact, what failure mode do you see that's giving that away?

345
00:14:55,690 --> 00:14:57,490
Let's go here.

346
00:14:57,490 --> 00:15:00,778
AUDIENCE: It has pink down the paint-- the cup.

347
00:15:00,778 --> 00:15:01,570
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes.

348
00:15:01,570 --> 00:15:03,970
AUDIENCE: So it might not know what color its should be.

349
00:15:03,970 --> 00:15:04,630
PHILLIP ISOLA: So it doesn't know.

350
00:15:04,630 --> 00:15:05,670
So it's oscillating.

351
00:15:05,670 --> 00:15:06,170
Exactly.

352
00:15:06,170 --> 00:15:07,128
There's pink down here.

353
00:15:07,128 --> 00:15:08,330
And there's blue here.

354
00:15:08,330 --> 00:15:11,730
And that's exactly this effect.

355
00:15:11,730 --> 00:15:14,250
This system is making independent predictions

356
00:15:14,250 --> 00:15:15,290
per pixel.

357
00:15:15,290 --> 00:15:17,910
And it crossed the threshold of probability.

358
00:15:17,910 --> 00:15:19,500
And it switched to the other mode.

359
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:21,750
It thinks this is maybe red paint or maybe blue paint.

360
00:15:21,750 --> 00:15:23,630
It's pretty sure it's one or the other of those things,

361
00:15:23,630 --> 00:15:25,213
but it doesn't know exactly which one.

362
00:15:25,213 --> 00:15:27,450
And then it just chaotically oscillates

363
00:15:27,450 --> 00:15:32,140
when the probability is very, very slightly.

364
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:36,620
Anyone see any other issues that may-- that give it away?

365
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:37,280
Let's go here.

366
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:37,780
Yeah?

367
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:40,238
AUDIENCE: There's a colored shadow underneath it.

368
00:15:40,238 --> 00:15:41,780
PHILLIP ISOLA: So I think that's just

369
00:15:41,780 --> 00:15:46,060
the resolution of the convolutional network that ran.

370
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:49,200
It just output a low-resolution prediction.

371
00:15:49,200 --> 00:15:52,020
So there's other artifacts like that, too.

372
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:53,100
How about this one?

373
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:56,660
This is Margaret Hamilton, who was working at MIT doing

374
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:59,980
programming in the '60s for the Apollo project.

375
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:03,820
So is this a real photo of her or a colorized photo

376
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:07,160
of her, a real color photo or synthetic?

377
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:09,700
So real-- raise your hand.

378
00:16:09,700 --> 00:16:12,297
A few more people-- fake.

379
00:16:12,297 --> 00:16:13,880
Actually, this is split half and half.

380
00:16:13,880 --> 00:16:15,420
Good.

381
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:17,220
It was fake, it turns out.

382
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:18,600
It was a black and white photo.

383
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:21,980
So who sees some issues that might give that away?

384
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:22,700
Over here?

385
00:16:22,700 --> 00:16:24,457
AUDIENCE: Were there [INAUDIBLE]?

386
00:16:24,457 --> 00:16:25,540
PHILLIP ISOLA: There were.

387
00:16:25,540 --> 00:16:27,740
There were color pictures in the '60s.

388
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:29,660
So it was only 1960s.

389
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:31,750
Sorry.

390
00:16:31,750 --> 00:16:32,932
Way in the back over here?

391
00:16:32,932 --> 00:16:35,390
AUDIENCE: There's a watermark in the bottom right that says

392
00:16:35,390 --> 00:16:36,182
"COLOR_BYANGELINA."

393
00:16:36,182 --> 00:16:38,230
[LAUGHTER]

394
00:16:38,230 --> 00:16:39,730
PHILLIP ISOLA: That I didn't notice.

395
00:16:39,730 --> 00:16:40,230
Good.

396
00:16:40,230 --> 00:16:41,830
So there's a watermark.

397
00:16:41,830 --> 00:16:42,790
Any others?

398
00:16:42,790 --> 00:16:44,450
And then way back?

399
00:16:44,450 --> 00:16:50,050
AUDIENCE: The door has some weird dark coloring on it.

400
00:16:50,050 --> 00:16:51,550
PHILLIP ISOLA: So it's a little hard

401
00:16:51,550 --> 00:16:53,490
to see from-- on this projector.

402
00:16:53,490 --> 00:16:55,510
But the door, and also the background here,

403
00:16:55,510 --> 00:16:58,270
is this mottled white-to-blue.

404
00:16:58,270 --> 00:17:01,630
And this is, again, that flipping around the probability

405
00:17:01,630 --> 00:17:03,150
thresholds.

406
00:17:03,150 --> 00:17:05,910
So it's not realizing that a white surface,

407
00:17:05,910 --> 00:17:08,012
like the paint on the wall, isn't-- is--

408
00:17:08,012 --> 00:17:08,970
has a joint dependency.

409
00:17:08,970 --> 00:17:11,190
If part of the paint is white, the rest of the paint

410
00:17:11,190 --> 00:17:13,310
should probably be white, too.

411
00:17:13,310 --> 00:17:14,869
There's one other issue that--

412
00:17:14,869 --> 00:17:17,670
so this is the issue of that.

413
00:17:17,670 --> 00:17:25,230
But there's one other issue, which is this issue here.

414
00:17:25,230 --> 00:17:29,190
So that issue is also showing up in this problem.

415
00:17:29,190 --> 00:17:31,990
So notice that all the colors look a little desaturated.

416
00:17:31,990 --> 00:17:37,070
It's because we're basically doing some averaging that

417
00:17:37,070 --> 00:17:39,072
is saying for all the possible colors,

418
00:17:39,072 --> 00:17:41,030
we'll collapse the distribution toward the mean

419
00:17:41,030 --> 00:17:43,070
because we're not sure what to output.

420
00:17:43,070 --> 00:17:46,830
So you would only know that the system is doing that if you know

421
00:17:46,830 --> 00:17:48,870
how that neural net is trained and what

422
00:17:48,870 --> 00:17:50,470
its objective function is.

423
00:17:50,470 --> 00:17:51,530
I happen to know that.

424
00:17:51,530 --> 00:17:56,110
But this is just one of the things you'll see all the time

425
00:17:56,110 --> 00:17:59,310
in AI-generated work, at least from a few years ago--

426
00:17:59,310 --> 00:18:01,210
that things look a little bit blurry,

427
00:18:01,210 --> 00:18:03,670
a little bit desaturated.

428
00:18:03,670 --> 00:18:05,410
And if you're in an audio domain,

429
00:18:05,410 --> 00:18:06,830
it might be everything looks--

430
00:18:06,830 --> 00:18:09,630
the music that's being generated sounds a little bit fuzzy.

431
00:18:09,630 --> 00:18:11,230
It's not crisp.

432
00:18:11,230 --> 00:18:13,190
These are the effect of averaging

433
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:17,550
that you get when you're fitting a distribution that doesn't have

434
00:18:17,550 --> 00:18:20,570
the capacity to approximate the true distribution

435
00:18:20,570 --> 00:18:24,150
and averages between all of the different possibilities

436
00:18:24,150 --> 00:18:25,370
in the true distribution.

437
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:31,660
So let's go back to 2012.

438
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:35,040
This is when deep learning really took off, became popular.

439
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:38,520
And I think that the story in 2012

440
00:18:38,520 --> 00:18:41,760
was very much about deep learning as a new hypothesis

441
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:44,080
space with high capacity.

442
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:45,920
So remember that in machine learning,

443
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:48,863
we always have my objective function, the hypothesis space

444
00:18:48,863 --> 00:18:50,280
I search over, and how do I search

445
00:18:50,280 --> 00:18:54,040
over that hypothesis space, which is called optimization.

446
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:55,488
So you would have seen this slide.

447
00:18:55,488 --> 00:18:56,780
Maybe some of you have seen it.

448
00:18:56,780 --> 00:19:00,360
I think it still shows up in various AI courses.

449
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:02,960
This was a very popular slide in 2012 or a little bit

450
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:04,720
after from Andrew Ng.

451
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:09,040
And he was pointing out that learning algorithms pre-deep

452
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:12,000
learning-- in those days, it might have been a linear support

453
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:12,740
vector machine.

454
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:15,700
Something that's linear is going to have less capacity.

455
00:19:15,700 --> 00:19:20,440
It's just a hypothesis space that can't fit nonlinear data.

456
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:22,340
And that means that as I get more data,

457
00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:23,980
then I'll find the best linear fit.

458
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:27,570
But then I won't ever be able to model the nonlinear

459
00:19:27,570 --> 00:19:28,332
parts of the data.

460
00:19:28,332 --> 00:19:30,790
And so deep learning will just keep scaling with more data,

461
00:19:30,790 --> 00:19:32,930
but other methods don't.

462
00:19:32,930 --> 00:19:36,070
So this is an argument about the hypothesis space.

463
00:19:38,770 --> 00:19:42,450
But I want to make an argument about the objective function.

464
00:19:42,450 --> 00:19:45,330
So let's go to this problem now, which is going to be,

465
00:19:45,330 --> 00:19:48,270
assign a class per pixel in an image.

466
00:19:48,270 --> 00:19:49,990
So recognize, is it the sky?

467
00:19:49,990 --> 00:19:50,550
Is it a bird?

468
00:19:50,550 --> 00:19:52,690
What is going on in this image?

469
00:19:52,690 --> 00:20:01,050
And we're just going to do the per-pixel classification.

470
00:20:01,050 --> 00:20:05,750
So let's see.

471
00:20:08,870 --> 00:20:09,670
So it works.

472
00:20:09,670 --> 00:20:11,990
But actually, let me go to this example.

473
00:20:11,990 --> 00:20:13,970
I think this will-- this is the better one.

474
00:20:13,970 --> 00:20:16,490
So let's suppose that I am trying

475
00:20:16,490 --> 00:20:20,490
to take a map of the world and predict,

476
00:20:20,490 --> 00:20:23,130
what does the landscape look like at that location

477
00:20:23,130 --> 00:20:24,090
in the world?

478
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:25,230
So this is a Google Map.

479
00:20:25,230 --> 00:20:28,810
And this is a satellite photo from Google Maps as well.

480
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:30,083
So I have paired data.

481
00:20:30,083 --> 00:20:31,750
I can just predict the y's from the x's.

482
00:20:31,750 --> 00:20:34,930
But I'm regressing or outputting an entire image.

483
00:20:34,930 --> 00:20:38,170
And now let's say that I use my unstructured prediction

484
00:20:38,170 --> 00:20:40,810
objective, which is to just minimize the squared error

485
00:20:40,810 --> 00:20:42,610
between my output.

486
00:20:42,610 --> 00:20:45,870
I can just take my output image, flatten it into a big vector,

487
00:20:45,870 --> 00:20:48,090
and just take the squared error between that vector

488
00:20:48,090 --> 00:20:50,063
and the ground truth vector.

489
00:20:50,063 --> 00:20:51,730
So this is like least squares regression

490
00:20:51,730 --> 00:20:53,690
for outputting a high-dimensional object.

491
00:20:53,690 --> 00:21:00,610
And this is implicitly treating all of the output dimensions as

492
00:21:00,610 --> 00:21:01,270
independent.

493
00:21:01,270 --> 00:21:03,270
That's basically what this sum is doing.

494
00:21:03,270 --> 00:21:06,370
So squared error-- remember, it's like log probability

495
00:21:06,370 --> 00:21:08,650
under a Gaussian probability model.

496
00:21:08,650 --> 00:21:11,470
Sum of logs is like the product of those probabilities.

497
00:21:11,470 --> 00:21:17,010
So this is implicitly a unstructured objective

498
00:21:17,010 --> 00:21:20,410
where I've assumed that the world factorizes independently

499
00:21:20,410 --> 00:21:22,700
over dimensions of the output space, independently

500
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:24,540
over each pixel.

501
00:21:24,540 --> 00:21:26,000
So I could do that.

502
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:30,820
And then I could run my greatest neural network of 2012

503
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:32,380
on this data.

504
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:34,180
I can make the biggest, highest capacity

505
00:21:34,180 --> 00:21:35,600
convolutional network I want.

506
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:38,440
I can use SGD to optimize it.

507
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:40,300
And here's the input.

508
00:21:40,300 --> 00:21:41,440
And here's the output.

509
00:21:44,126 --> 00:21:46,820
So it looks OK.

510
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:49,040
This is some buildings.

511
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:52,260
And they're getting colored a nice, bright paint

512
00:21:52,260 --> 00:21:53,820
like a city might have.

513
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:56,340
And the roads are asphalt. And they're gray.

514
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:57,475
But it looks blurry.

515
00:21:57,475 --> 00:21:58,600
So why does it look blurry?

516
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:00,860
Exactly the same reason again.

517
00:22:00,860 --> 00:22:03,220
This park has trees in it.

518
00:22:03,220 --> 00:22:05,480
Now, I don't know if it's an oak tree or a pine tree.

519
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:06,400
I don't know where the trees are.

520
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:08,140
If I average over all the different trees and all

521
00:22:08,140 --> 00:22:10,307
the different grass and all the different locations,

522
00:22:10,307 --> 00:22:13,540
I'm just going to make an average green in the park.

523
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:20,310
So by not modeling the joint dependencies between this region

524
00:22:20,310 --> 00:22:23,810
and this region, I have to--

525
00:22:23,810 --> 00:22:26,193
or basically, by not modeling the distribution

526
00:22:26,193 --> 00:22:28,110
over possibilities, I have to take the average

527
00:22:28,110 --> 00:22:29,068
over all possibilities.

528
00:22:29,068 --> 00:22:31,230
And that's a blurry image.

529
00:22:31,230 --> 00:22:37,350
So that was deep learning 2012 to 2016.

530
00:22:37,350 --> 00:22:41,510
And then deep learning 2016 to 2024

531
00:22:41,510 --> 00:22:44,830
has been about that, to some degree, but more about modifying

532
00:22:44,830 --> 00:22:46,170
now the objective function.

533
00:22:46,170 --> 00:22:48,830
And that's where the generative AI comes in.

534
00:22:48,830 --> 00:22:50,850
So we need an objective function,

535
00:22:50,850 --> 00:22:54,910
some way of penalizing my predictions, that

536
00:22:54,910 --> 00:22:58,150
can represent and model and penalize errors in structure,

537
00:22:58,150 --> 00:23:02,230
in high joint structure between pixels and the deviations

538
00:23:02,230 --> 00:23:09,710
from a multimodal posterior, both those kinds of structure

539
00:23:09,710 --> 00:23:14,650
So let's look at how to model that structure with a GAN.

540
00:23:14,650 --> 00:23:17,710
And then we'll look at how to model that structure with a VAE.

541
00:23:17,710 --> 00:23:20,510
And I'm going to talk about image-to-image problems

542
00:23:20,510 --> 00:23:25,990
in this section-- so input image, output predicted image.

543
00:23:25,990 --> 00:23:29,030
So first, we'll talk about GANs that are conditioned on an input

544
00:23:29,030 --> 00:23:30,670
image.

545
00:23:30,670 --> 00:23:33,590
So here is one way that you can do this.

546
00:23:33,590 --> 00:23:35,910
I'm switching the problem now to be--

547
00:23:35,910 --> 00:23:39,450
let's say that I have an architectural layout diagram.

548
00:23:39,450 --> 00:23:41,110
Each different color in the left image

549
00:23:41,110 --> 00:23:43,150
there is going to be indicating what

550
00:23:43,150 --> 00:23:45,050
is the architectural element-- is it a door,

551
00:23:45,050 --> 00:23:46,790
is it a window, and so forth.

552
00:23:46,790 --> 00:23:51,590
And my output prediction is what that building would look like.

553
00:23:51,590 --> 00:23:53,990
So I could be an architect trying to design new buildings

554
00:23:53,990 --> 00:23:55,670
and use this tool.

555
00:23:55,670 --> 00:23:57,150
I'll use a neural net.

556
00:23:57,150 --> 00:23:59,610
It could be a ConvNet in this project.

557
00:23:59,610 --> 00:24:02,710
But it could be a ViT in the modern era.

558
00:24:02,710 --> 00:24:05,350
And I will make my prediction.

559
00:24:05,350 --> 00:24:08,813
So I told you about GANs.

560
00:24:08,813 --> 00:24:10,230
When I told you about GANs, I said

561
00:24:10,230 --> 00:24:12,815
that we're going to take a random latent variable that's

562
00:24:12,815 --> 00:24:15,190
Gaussian-distributed, pass it through a generator, output

563
00:24:15,190 --> 00:24:18,120
an image such that a discriminator network can't

564
00:24:18,120 --> 00:24:20,060
tell whether that image is a real image

565
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:23,840
or a synthetic image generated by the generator.

566
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:27,920
Here, the noise is replaced with a conditioning variable.

567
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:29,900
So there doesn't have to be noise.

568
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:33,600
In this example, there is just a mapping from an x to a y

569
00:24:33,600 --> 00:24:35,140
as opposed to a z to an x.

570
00:24:38,250 --> 00:24:41,900
So it's the same exact math as the GAN.

571
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:44,760
G tries to synthesize fake images that fool d.

572
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:46,940
D tries to identify the fakes.

573
00:24:46,940 --> 00:24:48,920
They play this adversarial game.

574
00:24:48,920 --> 00:24:51,200
And at equilibrium, you're outputting

575
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:52,800
images that are indistinguishable

576
00:24:52,800 --> 00:24:55,600
to the discriminator from real images.

577
00:24:55,600 --> 00:24:57,082
So let's go through the math.

578
00:24:57,082 --> 00:24:59,540
I showed this very briefly in one of the previous lectures.

579
00:24:59,540 --> 00:25:03,160
But let's go through it again in the conditional setting.

580
00:25:03,160 --> 00:25:07,000
So here's the game from the perspective of the discriminator

581
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:07,942
network.

582
00:25:07,942 --> 00:25:09,400
What it's going to try to do-- it's

583
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:15,240
going to try to assign-- it's a binary cross-entropy objective.

584
00:25:15,240 --> 00:25:18,240
So that means it's going to try to assign high log

585
00:25:18,240 --> 00:25:21,680
probability to--

586
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:23,720
this is going to be like a fakeness classifier,

587
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:26,200
or it is a synthetic data classifier--

588
00:25:26,200 --> 00:25:29,260
will try to classify fake data as synthetic.

589
00:25:29,260 --> 00:25:34,960
So it'll try to assign high log probability to data which

590
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:36,743
is output by the generator.

591
00:25:36,743 --> 00:25:37,660
That's the definition.

592
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:40,120
Synthetic data is data output by the generator.

593
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:43,080
And it'll try to assign low log probability, 1

594
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:47,080
minus the probability, to real data, y.

595
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:49,100
So the training data, y, is real.

596
00:25:49,100 --> 00:25:49,860
The inputs are x.

597
00:25:49,860 --> 00:25:50,610
The outputs are y.

598
00:25:50,610 --> 00:25:51,640
That's the real data.

599
00:25:51,640 --> 00:25:53,782
I'm trying to distinguish between data

600
00:25:53,782 --> 00:25:55,240
that's created by g and data that's

601
00:25:55,240 --> 00:25:57,680
created by the true world, y.

602
00:25:57,680 --> 00:25:59,900
So this should be assigned high probability.

603
00:25:59,900 --> 00:26:06,220
It's just a binary classifier using cross-entropy objective.

604
00:26:06,220 --> 00:26:12,810
So that's the job of d, just classify synthetic or real.

605
00:26:12,810 --> 00:26:17,510
The job of g is to try to make synthetic images that look real

606
00:26:17,510 --> 00:26:19,690
according to d, that fool d.

607
00:26:19,690 --> 00:26:25,370
So d is trying to maximize the log probability

608
00:26:25,370 --> 00:26:28,370
that it places on the true data under this cross-entropy

609
00:26:28,370 --> 00:26:29,570
objective.

610
00:26:29,570 --> 00:26:32,230
And g is trying to do exactly the opposite,

611
00:26:32,230 --> 00:26:37,170
minimize the log probability that-- of a synthetic image

612
00:26:37,170 --> 00:26:39,090
being called synthetic.

613
00:26:39,090 --> 00:26:41,970
So it's basically trying to say, I want my synthetic data

614
00:26:41,970 --> 00:26:44,330
to cause d to be wrong--

615
00:26:44,330 --> 00:26:49,370
so minimizing as opposed to maximizing.

616
00:26:49,370 --> 00:26:56,010
So then we will backprop from d through the parameters of g,

617
00:26:56,010 --> 00:26:57,910
update g to make a more realistic photo.

618
00:26:57,910 --> 00:27:00,150
Now it becomes a real-looking photo.

619
00:27:00,150 --> 00:27:01,670
It doesn't have as many artifacts.

620
00:27:04,310 --> 00:27:07,190
So the GAN objective is this minimax game,

621
00:27:07,190 --> 00:27:11,700
this adversarial game where I'm trying to find the parameters

622
00:27:11,700 --> 00:27:15,300
of the data generator that will create outputs that

623
00:27:15,300 --> 00:27:18,360
a discriminator cannot tell whether they're real or fake.

624
00:27:18,360 --> 00:27:23,340
The discriminator will get a score of 0 or--

625
00:27:23,340 --> 00:27:25,120
as low a score as it possibly can.

626
00:27:27,850 --> 00:27:30,960
And we don't want to fool just any old random discriminator.

627
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:32,960
We want to fool the best possible discriminator.

628
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:34,780
So the discriminator is learning to get better and better

629
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:35,983
at detecting the fakes.

630
00:27:35,983 --> 00:27:37,900
And the generator is getting better and better

631
00:27:37,900 --> 00:27:39,120
at fooling the discriminator.

632
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:41,100
And at equilibrium, you can prove

633
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,100
that this will converge to being-- samples

634
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:46,960
from g are identically distributed as samples from y,

635
00:27:46,960 --> 00:27:49,580
the true data-generating process.

636
00:27:49,580 --> 00:27:51,220
So that's provable under the condition

637
00:27:51,220 --> 00:27:54,060
of the network has actually found the equilibrium-- the Nash

638
00:27:54,060 --> 00:27:56,020
equilibrium of this game and that there's

639
00:27:56,020 --> 00:27:57,800
sufficient capacity, and so forth.

640
00:27:57,800 --> 00:27:59,920
But the Nash equilibrium has that characteristic.

641
00:28:02,850 --> 00:28:06,620
So the perspective that I want to give in this lecture

642
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:13,580
is that from g's perspective, d is like a loss function.

643
00:28:13,580 --> 00:28:14,760
It is some kind of penalty.

644
00:28:14,760 --> 00:28:16,135
It's a critic or a discriminator.

645
00:28:16,135 --> 00:28:18,400
It's a penalty that looks at the outputs and says,

646
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:20,640
are they-- how good are they or how bad are they?

647
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:23,380
And then you can backprop and cause g

648
00:28:23,380 --> 00:28:25,420
to update to make better outputs.

649
00:28:25,420 --> 00:28:27,200
So it's like a loss function.

650
00:28:27,200 --> 00:28:29,122
It's like the squared error loss function,

651
00:28:29,122 --> 00:28:30,580
except it has a new property, which

652
00:28:30,580 --> 00:28:33,340
is that it's learned as opposed to being hand-defined.

653
00:28:33,340 --> 00:28:36,060
So it's a loss function that can look at structure in the output

654
00:28:36,060 --> 00:28:38,678
and not just say, did I get every pixel

655
00:28:38,678 --> 00:28:41,220
independently correct, but also, did I get the edges correct?

656
00:28:41,220 --> 00:28:43,180
And did I get the higher order statistics of the output

657
00:28:43,180 --> 00:28:43,900
correct?

658
00:28:43,900 --> 00:28:46,180
And this is what makes it powerful.

659
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:48,180
And it's very hard to hand-define such a loss

660
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:48,680
function.

661
00:28:48,680 --> 00:28:50,760
But it can be learned via this game.

662
00:28:54,920 --> 00:28:58,420
So you might have noticed that there's one little error

663
00:28:58,420 --> 00:28:59,680
with this formulation.

664
00:28:59,680 --> 00:29:02,360
So I gave you the GAN objective.

665
00:29:02,360 --> 00:29:05,220
But for a conditional GAN, there's actually a problem.

666
00:29:05,220 --> 00:29:11,630
So if I output this photo, should d in this setting call

667
00:29:11,630 --> 00:29:16,670
that a real photo or a fake, synthetic--

668
00:29:16,670 --> 00:29:19,310
is it real, basically?

669
00:29:19,310 --> 00:29:20,450
So it is a real photo.

670
00:29:20,450 --> 00:29:21,450
I'll just tell you that.

671
00:29:21,450 --> 00:29:22,590
This is a real photo.

672
00:29:22,590 --> 00:29:25,148
But it doesn't match the input.

673
00:29:25,148 --> 00:29:27,190
So the one little trick to make a GAN conditional

674
00:29:27,190 --> 00:29:28,950
is you need to let the discriminator

675
00:29:28,950 --> 00:29:31,930
look at not only is the output real,

676
00:29:31,930 --> 00:29:34,370
but does the output match the input?

677
00:29:34,370 --> 00:29:39,270
So it's, is the pair x, y a real pair

678
00:29:39,270 --> 00:29:43,132
sampled from the data-generating process P data x, y?

679
00:29:43,132 --> 00:29:45,090
D has to look at both the input and the output.

680
00:29:45,090 --> 00:29:46,798
Now it can penalize whether the output is

681
00:29:46,798 --> 00:29:48,210
blurry and unrealistic-looking.

682
00:29:48,210 --> 00:29:50,212
But it can also penalize whether the output has

683
00:29:50,212 --> 00:29:51,670
the windows in the wrong place that

684
00:29:51,670 --> 00:29:55,850
don't match where the architect has drawn the windows here.

685
00:29:55,850 --> 00:29:58,570
So that's a very simple modification to the objective.

686
00:29:58,570 --> 00:30:01,750
D just has to be conditioned on-- it has to take as input

687
00:30:01,750 --> 00:30:03,470
the data x.

688
00:30:03,470 --> 00:30:07,480
And it's asking, did g of x create a realistic output

689
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:08,420
that looks like y?

690
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:12,100
And also, did g of x conform to the input

691
00:30:12,100 --> 00:30:15,320
so that the relationship between the input and the output

692
00:30:15,320 --> 00:30:19,360
matches the true data distribution?

693
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:21,160
Question?

694
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:23,983
AUDIENCE: What is the definition of x for the ground truth label?

695
00:30:23,983 --> 00:30:25,400
Are you assuming you have some way

696
00:30:25,400 --> 00:30:29,643
of generating one of those diagrams for [INAUDIBLE]?

697
00:30:29,643 --> 00:30:31,560
PHILLIP ISOLA: So what is the definition of x?

698
00:30:31,560 --> 00:30:32,620
X is given.

699
00:30:32,620 --> 00:30:34,600
So these diagrams-- you have a data

700
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:37,460
set of diagrams with photos.

701
00:30:37,460 --> 00:30:38,460
So you have paired data.

702
00:30:38,460 --> 00:30:41,320
You have supervised data, x, y pairs.

703
00:30:41,320 --> 00:30:41,960
Yeah, question?

704
00:30:41,960 --> 00:30:43,502
AUDIENCE: Conceptually, at what point

705
00:30:43,502 --> 00:30:45,097
are we doing the loss function?

706
00:30:45,097 --> 00:30:46,680
Is it like we generate the whole image

707
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:49,880
and then we see that, oh, it's real or fake, or at what point

708
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:52,423
is this game of adversarial happening?

709
00:30:52,423 --> 00:30:53,840
PHILLIP ISOLA: So at what point is

710
00:30:53,840 --> 00:30:57,160
this-- are we applying the loss function?

711
00:30:57,160 --> 00:30:59,937
So the key idea is I'm not trying

712
00:30:59,937 --> 00:31:01,020
to say real or fake photo.

713
00:31:01,020 --> 00:31:04,400
I'm trying to say real or fake pair of photos.

714
00:31:04,400 --> 00:31:07,835
So what you actually do when you run this is you take x.

715
00:31:07,835 --> 00:31:09,460
You run it through your neural network.

716
00:31:09,460 --> 00:31:10,860
It outputs a prediction.

717
00:31:10,860 --> 00:31:14,260
And then you run that prediction through another neural network,

718
00:31:14,260 --> 00:31:15,440
the discriminator.

719
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:16,960
And that scores your prediction.

720
00:31:16,960 --> 00:31:19,460
And then you backprop through that entire computation graph.

721
00:31:19,460 --> 00:31:25,400
So the computation graph is d of g of x.

722
00:31:25,400 --> 00:31:27,400
This slide is just saying that I can also

723
00:31:27,400 --> 00:31:30,760
add a regular old regression objective, which

724
00:31:30,760 --> 00:31:33,760
is just-- this is what regular regression would do-- would say,

725
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:37,160
is the output close to the ground truth label

726
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:41,600
y, or the ground truth image y, in a squared error sense?

727
00:31:41,600 --> 00:31:44,510
And that will produce these blurry outputs because it

728
00:31:44,510 --> 00:31:46,880
doesn't-- it treats every pixel as independent,

729
00:31:46,880 --> 00:31:48,600
conditioned on the input.

730
00:31:48,600 --> 00:31:53,000
But it can be added to this GAN objective for a little bit

731
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:55,735
better performance because the big issue with GANs--

732
00:31:55,735 --> 00:31:56,860
they're not as popular now.

733
00:31:56,860 --> 00:31:59,408
And the main reason was they weren't very stable.

734
00:31:59,408 --> 00:32:00,700
They didn't converge very well.

735
00:32:00,700 --> 00:32:03,450
This adversarial game is very hard to optimize.

736
00:32:03,450 --> 00:32:06,290
But this regular regression loss can very much

737
00:32:06,290 --> 00:32:07,590
stabilize that process.

738
00:32:12,390 --> 00:32:13,270
So that works.

739
00:32:13,270 --> 00:32:15,370
But this is not impressive.

740
00:32:15,370 --> 00:32:17,410
By now, we've seen that you can generate

741
00:32:17,410 --> 00:32:20,810
all kinds of photos conditioned on all kinds of things.

742
00:32:20,810 --> 00:32:22,730
But a few years ago, it was magic.

743
00:32:22,730 --> 00:32:26,330
But anyway, this works.

744
00:32:26,330 --> 00:32:29,970
So I want to mention one of the interesting considerations

745
00:32:29,970 --> 00:32:37,110
in using discriminators to penalize structure in your data,

746
00:32:37,110 --> 00:32:39,310
to penalize errors in the structure of your outputs.

747
00:32:39,310 --> 00:32:41,490
And that is that the architecture

748
00:32:41,490 --> 00:32:46,970
of the discriminator critically matters in what types of errors

749
00:32:46,970 --> 00:32:48,910
it is sensitive to and what types of errors

750
00:32:48,910 --> 00:32:51,370
it's not sensitive to.

751
00:32:51,370 --> 00:32:55,330
So one of the things that worked really well in this project that

752
00:32:55,330 --> 00:32:59,010
made these images look good for that era

753
00:32:59,010 --> 00:33:02,930
was that we used this discriminator architecture

754
00:33:02,930 --> 00:33:05,610
called a patch discriminator.

755
00:33:05,610 --> 00:33:07,690
What that means is that rather than just looking

756
00:33:07,690 --> 00:33:09,148
at-- having a neural net that looks

757
00:33:09,148 --> 00:33:12,130
at the entire photo at once and says, is it real or fake,

758
00:33:12,130 --> 00:33:14,650
we look at every patch of the photo

759
00:33:14,650 --> 00:33:17,730
and say whether that patch looks like a real patch

760
00:33:17,730 --> 00:33:20,152
or a fake patch.

761
00:33:20,152 --> 00:33:21,610
So this means that discriminator is

762
00:33:21,610 --> 00:33:23,610
going to slide-- it's going to be convolutional.

763
00:33:23,610 --> 00:33:25,550
So it'll just slide across the input image.

764
00:33:25,550 --> 00:33:27,930
And for every patch, it will be making a prediction.

765
00:33:27,930 --> 00:33:32,590
And this gives you-- you can use a very small network to do that.

766
00:33:32,590 --> 00:33:33,710
So that's one advantage.

767
00:33:33,710 --> 00:33:36,130
And additionally, you have more supervision

768
00:33:36,130 --> 00:33:39,770
because you have a-- your discriminator's being trained

769
00:33:39,770 --> 00:33:42,030
on a data set of patches.

770
00:33:42,030 --> 00:33:44,530
And there's much-- many more patches than there are images.

771
00:33:44,530 --> 00:33:48,090
So if I have 10 images, I might have 10 million patches.

772
00:33:48,090 --> 00:33:50,730
And that's just more data.

773
00:33:50,730 --> 00:33:53,930
So one question that you can ask--

774
00:33:53,930 --> 00:33:57,900
but this is-- that was just the architecture of that time.

775
00:33:57,900 --> 00:34:00,820
One fun note is I think that this architecture is still

776
00:34:00,820 --> 00:34:02,382
part of Stable Diffusion to this day.

777
00:34:02,382 --> 00:34:04,340
But maybe in the very latest Stable Diffusions,

778
00:34:04,340 --> 00:34:04,965
it disappeared.

779
00:34:04,965 --> 00:34:06,340
But it was for quite a few years.

780
00:34:06,340 --> 00:34:08,382
So if you have ever played with Stable Diffusion,

781
00:34:08,382 --> 00:34:10,540
you had the patch GAN discriminator in there.

782
00:34:10,540 --> 00:34:14,520
But anyway, this architecture could change.

783
00:34:14,520 --> 00:34:17,460
But the important point is that this architecture defines

784
00:34:17,460 --> 00:34:20,400
the properties of the loss and how it looks at structure.

785
00:34:20,400 --> 00:34:23,717
So what happens if I make the receptive field, the patch

786
00:34:23,717 --> 00:34:25,800
size that the discriminator is looking at, really,

787
00:34:25,800 --> 00:34:27,219
really small?

788
00:34:27,219 --> 00:34:30,659
So if I make it really, really small, just 1 pixel by 1 pixel,

789
00:34:30,659 --> 00:34:33,219
I get back to almost the same thing

790
00:34:33,219 --> 00:34:37,860
as least squares regression that-- because I'm

791
00:34:37,860 --> 00:34:40,420
letting my discriminator only look at one pixel at a time

792
00:34:40,420 --> 00:34:41,679
to decide whether it's real or fake.

793
00:34:41,679 --> 00:34:42,739
And then it looks at the next pixel

794
00:34:42,739 --> 00:34:44,120
and decides if it's real or fake.

795
00:34:44,120 --> 00:34:46,460
It just takes a sum over all the errors.

796
00:34:46,460 --> 00:34:51,060
And that is, essentially, factorizing my model

797
00:34:51,060 --> 00:34:54,139
into a set of independent predictions

798
00:34:54,139 --> 00:34:58,350
that are not jointly penalized.

799
00:34:58,350 --> 00:35:02,060
If I make the patch 16-by-16, now I can say, look,

800
00:35:02,060 --> 00:35:04,310
each of these predictions might have been pretty good,

801
00:35:04,310 --> 00:35:05,730
but it's blurry.

802
00:35:05,730 --> 00:35:07,730
Blurriness cannot be seen in a single pixel.

803
00:35:07,730 --> 00:35:10,890
You can only see blurriness with two pixels, two measurements.

804
00:35:10,890 --> 00:35:12,510
You say that it's not sharp enough.

805
00:35:12,510 --> 00:35:14,427
The difference between them is not big enough.

806
00:35:14,427 --> 00:35:16,630
It's the second-order statistic of the data.

807
00:35:16,630 --> 00:35:19,502
And if I make it even bigger, I can model higher and higher

808
00:35:19,502 --> 00:35:21,710
order statistics, statistics that depend on the joint

809
00:35:21,710 --> 00:35:24,270
configuration of many, many measurements,

810
00:35:24,270 --> 00:35:27,350
70-by-70 measurements.

811
00:35:27,350 --> 00:35:31,190
And then in this era, if I-- if we make the patch size too big,

812
00:35:31,190 --> 00:35:35,510
the whole image size, it worked worse because--

813
00:35:35,510 --> 00:35:38,750
essentially for the reason that the model-- discriminator

814
00:35:38,750 --> 00:35:41,370
had too many parameters relative to the number of data points.

815
00:35:41,370 --> 00:35:43,750
But in the more modern era, this might not

816
00:35:43,750 --> 00:35:45,945
be such an issue because we just have infinite data.

817
00:35:45,945 --> 00:35:47,570
But back then, we only had finite data.

818
00:35:47,570 --> 00:35:49,250
This was trained on 400 images.

819
00:35:52,330 --> 00:35:55,350
So the story in 2012 was that deep learning

820
00:35:55,350 --> 00:35:58,150
is a high-capacity hypothesis space that

821
00:35:58,150 --> 00:36:01,750
can-- that many, many functions are in the hypothesis space.

822
00:36:01,750 --> 00:36:04,370
And older algorithms had low capacity.

823
00:36:04,370 --> 00:36:08,510
So they just could not find complicated nonlinear functions.

824
00:36:08,510 --> 00:36:11,670
What I want to point out with the last few slides

825
00:36:11,670 --> 00:36:14,910
is that deep learning with unstructured objectives

826
00:36:14,910 --> 00:36:16,227
actually does saturate.

827
00:36:16,227 --> 00:36:18,310
So it could be that, yes, in the hypothesis space,

828
00:36:18,310 --> 00:36:19,670
there's the correct answer.

829
00:36:19,670 --> 00:36:23,010
It's a universal approximator.

830
00:36:23,010 --> 00:36:25,670
I just make it big enough, wide enough, deep enough.

831
00:36:25,670 --> 00:36:29,590
But if I'm only incentivizing with my objective function

832
00:36:29,590 --> 00:36:32,110
that it's good enough just to model

833
00:36:32,110 --> 00:36:34,950
the factorized distribution, treating

834
00:36:34,950 --> 00:36:37,315
every different dimension of the output as independent--

835
00:36:37,315 --> 00:36:38,690
if that's considered good enough,

836
00:36:38,690 --> 00:36:41,150
there'll be no incentive to model the joint dependencies.

837
00:36:41,150 --> 00:36:46,710
And you won't get a good result. But with objective functions,

838
00:36:46,710 --> 00:36:50,910
and all generative models have this objective function that

839
00:36:50,910 --> 00:36:53,020
can model joint dependencies in the output,

840
00:36:53,020 --> 00:36:56,440
you get better performance.

841
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:57,340
This is a cartoon.

842
00:36:57,340 --> 00:37:01,000
But these little swaps of the ordering

843
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:02,720
are meant to be indicative of the fact

844
00:37:02,720 --> 00:37:05,900
that with more capacity, you might require more data.

845
00:37:05,900 --> 00:37:09,920
So less capacity and a simpler set of modeling assumptions

846
00:37:09,920 --> 00:37:13,377
might mean that you can use less data to get a good result.

847
00:37:13,377 --> 00:37:14,960
But if you have more data, then you'll

848
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:18,680
get better results if you have more capacity and a more

849
00:37:18,680 --> 00:37:22,960
flexible model class.

850
00:37:22,960 --> 00:37:27,740
So here's where we were with just unstructured prediction.

851
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:30,440
Each pixel is independent, penalized with a least--

852
00:37:30,440 --> 00:37:32,400
an L1 loss per pixel.

853
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:35,200
And here's where we are with the patch GAN

854
00:37:35,200 --> 00:37:37,040
discriminator and the conditional GAN,

855
00:37:37,040 --> 00:37:38,780
where now it says, it was blurry.

856
00:37:38,780 --> 00:37:39,780
That looked unrealistic.

857
00:37:39,780 --> 00:37:40,697
That's obviously fake.

858
00:37:40,697 --> 00:37:42,720
I better add a bunch of trees.

859
00:37:42,720 --> 00:37:44,800
But we enter into the regime of hallucination,

860
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:47,080
where these trees are just a guess.

861
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:49,120
They're just a sample from the true distribution

862
00:37:49,120 --> 00:37:50,890
over where the trees might be.

863
00:37:50,890 --> 00:37:54,010
So it's not necessarily those trees are actually

864
00:37:54,010 --> 00:37:54,790
at that location.

865
00:37:54,790 --> 00:37:57,290
It's just this is one of the modes of the distribution

866
00:37:57,290 --> 00:37:58,270
that I'm modeling.

867
00:38:02,210 --> 00:38:03,030
Questions?

868
00:38:03,030 --> 00:38:04,963
Yeah?

869
00:38:04,963 --> 00:38:06,630
AUDIENCE: I don't know if you know this.

870
00:38:06,630 --> 00:38:09,250
But what would happen if you did an L2 loss that's

871
00:38:09,250 --> 00:38:12,250
like a Gaussian, but with covariance, where you enforce

872
00:38:12,250 --> 00:38:14,730
some structure in the data?

873
00:38:14,730 --> 00:38:16,930
PHILLIP ISOLA: Covariance over the full joint--

874
00:38:16,930 --> 00:38:19,410
just the full vector?

875
00:38:19,410 --> 00:38:21,630
AUDIENCE: Two pixels are close or pressed together,

876
00:38:21,630 --> 00:38:22,568
and that somehow--

877
00:38:22,568 --> 00:38:24,610
PHILLIP ISOLA: Like some kind of Gaussian process

878
00:38:24,610 --> 00:38:26,510
over the entire set of measurements,

879
00:38:26,510 --> 00:38:29,370
something like that, or just one big Gaussian?

880
00:38:29,370 --> 00:38:30,690
I think it will--

881
00:38:30,690 --> 00:38:33,090
it'll improve things over this result.

882
00:38:33,090 --> 00:38:35,210
But I would be pretty surprised if it

883
00:38:35,210 --> 00:38:37,130
makes a dramatic difference.

884
00:38:37,130 --> 00:38:40,550
I think that that still can only model Gaussian structure.

885
00:38:40,550 --> 00:38:44,290
And there might be other structures that you're missing.

886
00:38:44,290 --> 00:38:46,810
AUDIENCE: So the L1 prediction seems

887
00:38:46,810 --> 00:38:49,430
to very faithfully represent the map.

888
00:38:49,430 --> 00:38:52,330
But the-- looking carefully at the GAN representation

889
00:38:52,330 --> 00:38:54,910
seems to have eliminated its-- some of the details that are

890
00:38:54,910 --> 00:38:55,410
[INAUDIBLE].

891
00:38:55,410 --> 00:38:56,557
So is this a common thing?

892
00:38:56,557 --> 00:38:58,890
PHILLIP ISOLA: So the question is, the L1 reconstruction

893
00:38:58,890 --> 00:38:59,990
is very faithful.

894
00:38:59,990 --> 00:39:02,770
And the GAN-- actually, the-- some of the paths

895
00:39:02,770 --> 00:39:04,610
might not quite match, like maybe

896
00:39:04,610 --> 00:39:06,530
this road disappeared here.

897
00:39:06,530 --> 00:39:10,353
So this is a common trade-off-- that the L1 prediction

898
00:39:10,353 --> 00:39:11,270
is taking the average.

899
00:39:11,270 --> 00:39:12,228
It's very conservative.

900
00:39:12,228 --> 00:39:14,850
It's very robust in that particular sense

901
00:39:14,850 --> 00:39:17,970
that we can be pretty sure that there's

902
00:39:17,970 --> 00:39:20,450
going to be some road here.

903
00:39:20,450 --> 00:39:23,890
And all the modes of the distribution

904
00:39:23,890 --> 00:39:25,810
must share that so that the average will also

905
00:39:25,810 --> 00:39:28,510
share that road, whereas, again, we'll select a mode.

906
00:39:28,510 --> 00:39:29,830
And the mode should share it.

907
00:39:29,830 --> 00:39:34,810
But essentially, maybe this just--

908
00:39:34,810 --> 00:39:37,167
this mode is a little deviated from--

909
00:39:37,167 --> 00:39:38,750
I think it's kind of like an ensemble.

910
00:39:38,750 --> 00:39:40,850
An ensemble might be a more robust prediction

911
00:39:40,850 --> 00:39:45,463
of the truth than one of the modes that might

912
00:39:45,463 --> 00:39:46,630
be a little low probability.

913
00:39:46,630 --> 00:39:50,020
And maybe this is low probability in that region.

914
00:39:50,020 --> 00:39:53,940
So the rough idea is that the problem with generative AI stuff

915
00:39:53,940 --> 00:39:57,140
is that you often will get these hallucinations that are samples

916
00:39:57,140 --> 00:40:00,540
from how something might be, but not all aspects of that sample

917
00:40:00,540 --> 00:40:02,033
might actually be high-probability.

918
00:40:02,033 --> 00:40:04,200
And I think that's what you're seeing here, roughly.

919
00:40:07,880 --> 00:40:09,818
So let's go on to conditional VAEs.

920
00:40:09,818 --> 00:40:11,860
So every generative model that we've talked about

921
00:40:11,860 --> 00:40:12,960
can be made conditional.

922
00:40:12,960 --> 00:40:15,085
Oftentimes, it's quite easy to make it conditional.

923
00:40:15,085 --> 00:40:17,460
So let's see how to make a VAE conditional

924
00:40:17,460 --> 00:40:20,380
for the same problem.

925
00:40:20,380 --> 00:40:24,380
So one of the issues with the GAN formulation

926
00:40:24,380 --> 00:40:26,660
is that this generator function tends

927
00:40:26,660 --> 00:40:29,700
to exhibit what's called mode collapse, which is that, yes, it

928
00:40:29,700 --> 00:40:31,620
will output one mode of the distribution that

929
00:40:31,620 --> 00:40:32,860
looks realistic.

930
00:40:32,860 --> 00:40:35,327
But it won't-- it will maybe miss other modes

931
00:40:35,327 --> 00:40:36,160
of the distribution.

932
00:40:36,160 --> 00:40:37,380
It's not great at actually capturing

933
00:40:37,380 --> 00:40:38,980
the full posterior, just maybe at one

934
00:40:38,980 --> 00:40:41,140
or a few modes of the posterior.

935
00:40:41,140 --> 00:40:43,580
So conditional VAEs are a method that

936
00:40:43,580 --> 00:40:45,140
is a little better at maybe capturing

937
00:40:45,140 --> 00:40:47,340
all the modes of the posterior.

938
00:40:47,340 --> 00:40:49,500
And here's how they work.

939
00:40:49,500 --> 00:40:52,420
So what I meant to show here is that this schematic

940
00:40:52,420 --> 00:40:53,732
could match that building.

941
00:40:53,732 --> 00:40:55,440
But it also could match a brick building.

942
00:40:55,440 --> 00:40:57,178
I have no idea what the material is.

943
00:40:57,178 --> 00:40:59,220
It's not told to me in this schematic, just where

944
00:40:59,220 --> 00:41:02,260
the windows and doors are.

945
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:04,940
So what I'll do is I'll simply use this VAE trick where

946
00:41:04,940 --> 00:41:07,340
I'm going to have some latent variable z that will get

947
00:41:07,340 --> 00:41:10,220
injected into the network in order

948
00:41:10,220 --> 00:41:14,460
to model all of the uncertainty over the possible variations

949
00:41:14,460 --> 00:41:17,920
in the predictions.

950
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:21,180
So let me go to this very simple example.

951
00:41:21,180 --> 00:41:23,620
We have a pink square here.

952
00:41:23,620 --> 00:41:26,220
And it's moving up and to the right over time.

953
00:41:26,220 --> 00:41:28,552
So you can see that as I go to the right,

954
00:41:28,552 --> 00:41:31,260
the square is actually getting a little bit closer to the corner.

955
00:41:31,260 --> 00:41:33,047
And if I do--

956
00:41:33,047 --> 00:41:35,130
if I try to predict the future given the past-- so

957
00:41:35,130 --> 00:41:36,797
I'm going to take this frame of a video.

958
00:41:36,797 --> 00:41:37,820
This is a moving object.

959
00:41:37,820 --> 00:41:40,300
Where will it go next?

960
00:41:40,300 --> 00:41:43,350
If I do that with the least squares objective,

961
00:41:43,350 --> 00:41:46,610
then I am going to get averaging.

962
00:41:46,610 --> 00:41:48,110
So there's eight possible directions

963
00:41:48,110 --> 00:41:50,550
this square could go in this little demo.

964
00:41:50,550 --> 00:41:53,750
So here are the eight possible directions it could go.

965
00:41:53,750 --> 00:41:55,670
And my prediction will be an average

966
00:41:55,670 --> 00:41:57,990
of all eight possible directions, average of the eight

967
00:41:57,990 --> 00:41:59,910
modes.

968
00:41:59,910 --> 00:42:03,433
So we want to somehow be able to model

969
00:42:03,433 --> 00:42:05,350
each of the modes and sample each of the modes

970
00:42:05,350 --> 00:42:07,268
and not just sample the average.

971
00:42:07,268 --> 00:42:09,810
The average has zero probability under the data distribution.

972
00:42:09,810 --> 00:42:13,230
So that's a very bad prediction.

973
00:42:13,230 --> 00:42:17,390
So we're going to build up something that looks like a VAE.

974
00:42:17,390 --> 00:42:21,070
So first, we have what looks like an autoencoder or an--

975
00:42:21,070 --> 00:42:22,870
the VAEs we saw last class.

976
00:42:22,870 --> 00:42:25,470
We just are going to take my target.

977
00:42:25,470 --> 00:42:27,330
This is the prediction of the future.

978
00:42:27,330 --> 00:42:30,910
We're going to try to compress it into a bottleneck, a Gaussian

979
00:42:30,910 --> 00:42:36,470
variable z, and then reconstruct that prediction.

980
00:42:36,470 --> 00:42:38,830
So I'm trying to predict y given x.

981
00:42:38,830 --> 00:42:40,110
This is x.

982
00:42:40,110 --> 00:42:41,070
And this is y.

983
00:42:41,070 --> 00:42:44,400
Here, I'm just running a VAE on y.

984
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:46,820
Now, to make it a conditional VAE,

985
00:42:46,820 --> 00:42:50,460
I'm going to simply let my reconstruction network,

986
00:42:50,460 --> 00:42:54,320
my decoder, observe the data x.

987
00:42:54,320 --> 00:42:55,940
What it's going to try to do is say,

988
00:42:55,940 --> 00:43:01,600
I want to predict y, the future, from x.

989
00:43:01,600 --> 00:43:04,320
But I'm also going to tell you the answer.

990
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:07,280
But I'm going to force the answer, y,

991
00:43:07,280 --> 00:43:11,080
to go through an information bottleneck, z.

992
00:43:11,080 --> 00:43:15,600
So what's going to happen is this arrow here is going to say,

993
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:18,000
well, I don't know what direction it will go.

994
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:20,840
That information has to come from z.

995
00:43:20,840 --> 00:43:24,320
So z will learn to encode what direction this thing goes.

996
00:43:24,320 --> 00:43:25,683
But z is compressed.

997
00:43:25,683 --> 00:43:27,100
Z is trying to be a unit Gaussian.

998
00:43:27,100 --> 00:43:28,780
So it's trying to remove information.

999
00:43:28,780 --> 00:43:32,600
And so rather than encoding the color in z,

1000
00:43:32,600 --> 00:43:33,940
the color can be given by x.

1001
00:43:33,940 --> 00:43:37,618
And there's higher information capacity in this arrow.

1002
00:43:37,618 --> 00:43:39,160
So what will happen is this is trying

1003
00:43:39,160 --> 00:43:40,720
to learn as little as possible in order

1004
00:43:40,720 --> 00:43:41,820
to make the correct prediction.

1005
00:43:41,820 --> 00:43:43,320
So I don't need to encode the color,

1006
00:43:43,320 --> 00:43:45,780
since that's given in my observation.

1007
00:43:45,780 --> 00:43:49,200
So z ends up only encoding the direction

1008
00:43:49,200 --> 00:43:54,340
of motion, which is unobserved in the observation.

1009
00:43:54,340 --> 00:44:01,140
So if z sees this target, it will compress that into, oh,

1010
00:44:01,140 --> 00:44:04,040
it-- the box should go down.

1011
00:44:04,040 --> 00:44:06,760
So z learns to encode whatever information

1012
00:44:06,760 --> 00:44:08,920
is missing from the observation in order

1013
00:44:08,920 --> 00:44:10,740
to make a correct prediction.

1014
00:44:13,400 --> 00:44:17,720
Now, at sampling time, I can sample from this model

1015
00:44:17,720 --> 00:44:20,360
by replacing my encoding--

1016
00:44:20,360 --> 00:44:24,340
my inference of z given a target image with z as a unit Gaussian

1017
00:44:24,340 --> 00:44:26,000
because I've trained it as a VAE.

1018
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:28,500
And I've imposed that it should be close to a unit Gaussian.

1019
00:44:28,500 --> 00:44:30,160
So I can just sample from z.

1020
00:44:30,160 --> 00:44:31,420
And now I'll sample from z.

1021
00:44:31,420 --> 00:44:33,920
Maybe I sample the down z vector.

1022
00:44:33,920 --> 00:44:36,498
That will cause me to predict that the object goes down.

1023
00:44:36,498 --> 00:44:38,040
So I'm sampling all the possible ways

1024
00:44:38,040 --> 00:44:40,050
it can move by sampling from z.

1025
00:44:40,050 --> 00:44:40,910
Now I sample up.

1026
00:44:40,910 --> 00:44:41,470
It goes up.

1027
00:44:45,290 --> 00:44:46,850
It goes right.

1028
00:44:46,850 --> 00:44:50,030
So you can train this for all kinds of data.

1029
00:44:50,030 --> 00:44:53,090
On this example, I'm introducing these latent variables

1030
00:44:53,090 --> 00:44:56,850
that specify everything that's unspecified by the input x.

1031
00:44:56,850 --> 00:45:00,290
So z learns to model everything that's necessary in order

1032
00:45:00,290 --> 00:45:02,850
to pin down what the output should

1033
00:45:02,850 --> 00:45:07,670
look like that's not explicitly given in x, all the uncertainty.

1034
00:45:07,670 --> 00:45:10,242
So P of y given x is uncertain.

1035
00:45:10,242 --> 00:45:12,450
So I'm going to add a latent variable z, just like we

1036
00:45:12,450 --> 00:45:16,770
did with VAEs, where we're trying to learn all--

1037
00:45:16,770 --> 00:45:19,490
in unconditional VAEs, z has to encode everything

1038
00:45:19,490 --> 00:45:20,430
about the data.

1039
00:45:20,430 --> 00:45:22,410
In conditional VAEs, z only encodes

1040
00:45:22,410 --> 00:45:25,270
information that's not given in the conditioning variable, x.

1041
00:45:30,380 --> 00:45:35,570
So one concept to give you here is now

1042
00:45:35,570 --> 00:45:36,980
we really have two different ways

1043
00:45:36,980 --> 00:45:39,738
of controlling the outputs of a generative model.

1044
00:45:39,738 --> 00:45:42,280
In the previous lectures, we just had the latent variables z.

1045
00:45:42,280 --> 00:45:43,820
And there were knobs that specify

1046
00:45:43,820 --> 00:45:45,380
all the attributes of the data.

1047
00:45:45,380 --> 00:45:48,100
And those knobs are here, controlling

1048
00:45:48,100 --> 00:45:49,900
the lighting and the materials.

1049
00:45:49,900 --> 00:45:52,460
But now we also have conditioning variables, x.

1050
00:45:55,060 --> 00:45:58,260
And these also can control the way that the data looks.

1051
00:45:58,260 --> 00:46:01,042
And for a lot of modern generative models,

1052
00:46:01,042 --> 00:46:03,500
actually, the latent variables might play a very small role

1053
00:46:03,500 --> 00:46:06,100
because you're just conditioning on more and more data.

1054
00:46:06,100 --> 00:46:08,980
So in text-to-image models, if you tell it

1055
00:46:08,980 --> 00:46:10,900
a whole string of text, now you don't

1056
00:46:10,900 --> 00:46:14,340
need a lot of latent variables because the string almost-- very

1057
00:46:14,340 --> 00:46:17,300
precisely could pin down what you're going to see.

1058
00:46:17,300 --> 00:46:21,620
So let's look at text models next.

1059
00:46:21,620 --> 00:46:24,760
So first, we'll talk about text-to-text.

1060
00:46:24,760 --> 00:46:28,020
Luckily, text-to-text is basically nothing new

1061
00:46:28,020 --> 00:46:31,660
because text models, language models,

1062
00:46:31,660 --> 00:46:33,320
are typically autoregressive.

1063
00:46:33,320 --> 00:46:36,540
Let's look at the autoregressive class of language models.

1064
00:46:36,540 --> 00:46:38,260
Autoregressive models are already,

1065
00:46:38,260 --> 00:46:41,540
by construction, conditional generative

1066
00:46:41,540 --> 00:46:43,980
models because an autoregressive model--

1067
00:46:43,980 --> 00:46:46,620
if you recall, we model the data as a sequence

1068
00:46:46,620 --> 00:46:48,780
of conditional predictions.

1069
00:46:48,780 --> 00:46:51,300
The probability is a product over all of the conditionals P

1070
00:46:51,300 --> 00:46:56,780
of x2 given x1, P of x3 given x1 and x2, and so forth.

1071
00:46:56,780 --> 00:47:00,560
So autoregressive models are natively conditional.

1072
00:47:00,560 --> 00:47:04,660
And you don't have to modify them or do something new to turn

1073
00:47:04,660 --> 00:47:06,980
them into conditional models.

1074
00:47:06,980 --> 00:47:09,820
So let's look at this with a conditional language prediction

1075
00:47:09,820 --> 00:47:10,320
problem.

1076
00:47:10,320 --> 00:47:12,360
So conditional prediction is take text as input,

1077
00:47:12,360 --> 00:47:16,140
predict some other text as output So the way

1078
00:47:16,140 --> 00:47:18,563
you train it is the way we've always described

1079
00:47:18,563 --> 00:47:19,480
training these things.

1080
00:47:19,480 --> 00:47:21,700
You just do next-word prediction.

1081
00:47:21,700 --> 00:47:24,380
"Colorless green ideas sleep"-- what's the next character

1082
00:47:24,380 --> 00:47:25,580
or next word?

1083
00:47:25,580 --> 00:47:28,260
"Furiously."

1084
00:47:28,260 --> 00:47:33,550
And now I can use this to solve conditional prediction problems

1085
00:47:33,550 --> 00:47:38,330
by simply giving the language model whatever input I want.

1086
00:47:38,330 --> 00:47:40,590
And that's the conditioning variable, x.

1087
00:47:40,590 --> 00:47:43,227
The stochasticity of the latent variables of this model--

1088
00:47:43,227 --> 00:47:44,810
there's not explicit latent variables.

1089
00:47:44,810 --> 00:47:47,710
But it's stochastic because every next word I predict

1090
00:47:47,710 --> 00:47:50,870
will be sampled from a categorical distribution

1091
00:47:50,870 --> 00:47:55,070
over the next word if I'm doing a autoregressive model where

1092
00:47:55,070 --> 00:47:58,450
every conditional density is a categorical distribution.

1093
00:47:58,450 --> 00:48:00,150
So it's still stochastic.

1094
00:48:00,150 --> 00:48:02,590
But these conditioning variables are now made explicit

1095
00:48:02,590 --> 00:48:04,270
in the training process.

1096
00:48:04,270 --> 00:48:06,290
And you can do all kinds of things.

1097
00:48:06,290 --> 00:48:08,550
And this is often called prompting

1098
00:48:08,550 --> 00:48:11,855
language models, which I'm sure most of you have played with.

1099
00:48:11,855 --> 00:48:13,230
This is what you do when you just

1100
00:48:13,230 --> 00:48:16,790
talk with your favorite LLM, Claude or ChatGPT.

1101
00:48:16,790 --> 00:48:20,310
So let's say I want to turn my language model into a classifier

1102
00:48:20,310 --> 00:48:22,370
of the sentiment of a review.

1103
00:48:22,370 --> 00:48:23,390
I have a product review.

1104
00:48:23,390 --> 00:48:25,870
I want to now predict conditionally,

1105
00:48:25,870 --> 00:48:27,990
conditioned on the review, what--

1106
00:48:27,990 --> 00:48:30,030
did the user like it or not?

1107
00:48:30,030 --> 00:48:32,840
So here's how you do it with an autoregressive language model.

1108
00:48:32,840 --> 00:48:34,180
You simply take the review.

1109
00:48:34,180 --> 00:48:39,340
You concatenate, you prefix that onto the input to your question.

1110
00:48:39,340 --> 00:48:41,720
So that's like conditioning on x the review.

1111
00:48:41,720 --> 00:48:43,760
And then you add whatever question you want,

1112
00:48:43,760 --> 00:48:45,760
like this-- what's the sentiment in this review,

1113
00:48:45,760 --> 00:48:47,320
or the sentiment in this review is,

1114
00:48:47,320 --> 00:48:48,760
and then the next-word prediction

1115
00:48:48,760 --> 00:48:50,300
should be your predicted y.

1116
00:48:50,300 --> 00:48:52,400
So it's P of y given x, where x is the review

1117
00:48:52,400 --> 00:48:55,960
and y is the sentiment--

1118
00:48:55,960 --> 00:49:00,760
so really easy way to do all kinds of conditional predictions

1119
00:49:00,760 --> 00:49:02,880
with language models.

1120
00:49:02,880 --> 00:49:08,960
This became evident, I would say, in the GPT-3 era.

1121
00:49:08,960 --> 00:49:11,198
Now this is-- people use language models this way

1122
00:49:11,198 --> 00:49:11,740
all the time.

1123
00:49:11,740 --> 00:49:13,600
But it was-- it took a while for folks

1124
00:49:13,600 --> 00:49:16,280
to realize that language models are not

1125
00:49:16,280 --> 00:49:18,940
just about writing paragraphs from scratch,

1126
00:49:18,940 --> 00:49:20,620
like writing funny stories from scratch.

1127
00:49:20,620 --> 00:49:23,140
They're actually most powerful for conditional prediction.

1128
00:49:23,140 --> 00:49:25,420
Given an instruction or a prompt or a question,

1129
00:49:25,420 --> 00:49:27,020
they can answer that question for you.

1130
00:49:27,020 --> 00:49:28,603
It's a conditional prediction problem.

1131
00:49:28,603 --> 00:49:30,800
And that's where they really became powerful.

1132
00:49:30,800 --> 00:49:31,840
So here, I gave Chat--

1133
00:49:31,840 --> 00:49:34,820
I gave GPT-3 this review.

1134
00:49:34,820 --> 00:49:36,597
And then it says the next character--

1135
00:49:36,597 --> 00:49:38,180
or the next word should be "negative."

1136
00:49:38,180 --> 00:49:41,620
And that's a prediction of the sentiment in the review.

1137
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:46,640
Question, yeah?

1138
00:49:46,640 --> 00:49:48,106
AUDIENCE: Is there a reason "five"

1139
00:49:48,106 --> 00:49:50,410
has underscores [INAUDIBLE] it?

1140
00:49:52,190 --> 00:49:52,940
PHILLIP ISOLA: No.

1141
00:49:52,940 --> 00:49:56,960
I think that just people put italics with two underscores.

1142
00:49:56,960 --> 00:49:58,738
Just, it was how that review was written.

1143
00:49:58,738 --> 00:49:59,280
I don't know.

1144
00:49:59,280 --> 00:50:01,300
I think I probably made this one up myself.

1145
00:50:01,300 --> 00:50:04,520
So I was just indicating italics.

1146
00:50:04,520 --> 00:50:05,680
It doesn't really matter.

1147
00:50:05,680 --> 00:50:06,400
Yep?

1148
00:50:06,400 --> 00:50:10,440
AUDIENCE: Is this similar to retrieval-augmented generation?

1149
00:50:10,440 --> 00:50:13,200
PHILLIP ISOLA: So retrieval-augmented generation

1150
00:50:13,200 --> 00:50:15,360
would--

1151
00:50:15,360 --> 00:50:16,220
is related to this.

1152
00:50:16,220 --> 00:50:21,160
But I would say for now, treat that as a separate concept.

1153
00:50:21,160 --> 00:50:25,140
There's no explicit retrieval in what I've shown so far.

1154
00:50:25,140 --> 00:50:25,640
Yeah?

1155
00:50:25,640 --> 00:50:27,598
AUDIENCE: In this case, for training, do I just

1156
00:50:27,598 --> 00:50:29,610
generate a bunch of instructions and try

1157
00:50:29,610 --> 00:50:33,298
to make the GPT answer to these instructions?

1158
00:50:33,298 --> 00:50:35,090
PHILLIP ISOLA: How do you train this thing?

1159
00:50:35,090 --> 00:50:36,550
So there's two ways.

1160
00:50:36,550 --> 00:50:38,610
So one is that you just--

1161
00:50:38,610 --> 00:50:42,650
you're modeling all of the words that any human has ever spoken

1162
00:50:42,650 --> 00:50:45,610
or all the words on the internet or some huge corpus

1163
00:50:45,610 --> 00:50:46,930
of sentences.

1164
00:50:46,930 --> 00:50:52,370
And probability of sentiment given review

1165
00:50:52,370 --> 00:50:56,050
might be somewhere in that space of all possible things people

1166
00:50:56,050 --> 00:50:58,410
have ever talked about.

1167
00:50:58,410 --> 00:51:00,350
When I'm just training a language model,

1168
00:51:00,350 --> 00:51:02,225
it will learn all these different conditional

1169
00:51:02,225 --> 00:51:04,610
distributions if they exist in the data.

1170
00:51:04,610 --> 00:51:07,330
So this is one conditional that exists

1171
00:51:07,330 --> 00:51:10,690
in the data, the conditional of predicting the next sentiment

1172
00:51:10,690 --> 00:51:15,370
given the previous paragraph.

1173
00:51:15,370 --> 00:51:19,530
But now if your conditional distribution you want to model

1174
00:51:19,530 --> 00:51:22,530
is not just implicit in the data,

1175
00:51:22,530 --> 00:51:25,290
then you can collect your own data set of reviews

1176
00:51:25,290 --> 00:51:27,728
and sentiment labels.

1177
00:51:27,728 --> 00:51:29,270
And then you could fine-tune on that.

1178
00:51:32,970 --> 00:51:33,470
Cool.

1179
00:51:33,470 --> 00:51:36,010
So we'll have another lecture on language models

1180
00:51:36,010 --> 00:51:38,552
where we go a little bit more into all the different ways you

1181
00:51:38,552 --> 00:51:40,930
can prompt and tune and adapt these things.

1182
00:51:40,930 --> 00:51:42,450
But basically, autoregressive models

1183
00:51:42,450 --> 00:51:45,890
are just conditional by default.

1184
00:51:45,890 --> 00:51:47,730
So now let's look at an autoregressive model

1185
00:51:47,730 --> 00:51:48,928
for image-to-text.

1186
00:51:48,928 --> 00:51:50,470
So again, it should be pretty simple.

1187
00:51:50,470 --> 00:51:53,010
But we need to make sure we can map between two different data

1188
00:51:53,010 --> 00:51:53,510
types.

1189
00:51:53,510 --> 00:51:55,590
And the way we do that is we use transformers,

1190
00:51:55,590 --> 00:51:58,330
which are a way of putting all data types

1191
00:51:58,330 --> 00:51:59,630
onto the same equal footing.

1192
00:51:59,630 --> 00:52:01,005
Just turn everything into tokens,

1193
00:52:01,005 --> 00:52:03,610
and then just do token-to-token mappings.

1194
00:52:03,610 --> 00:52:05,610
So I'm showing images.

1195
00:52:05,610 --> 00:52:06,430
I'm showing text.

1196
00:52:06,430 --> 00:52:08,610
But whatever data format you want to work with--

1197
00:52:08,610 --> 00:52:11,930
molecules, financial data, spreadsheets--

1198
00:52:11,930 --> 00:52:15,570
if you just can convert it to tokens,

1199
00:52:15,570 --> 00:52:18,310
little bundles of information with an embedding,

1200
00:52:18,310 --> 00:52:21,560
a token vector, then you'll be able to work--

1201
00:52:21,560 --> 00:52:23,810
you'll be able to run all the same technology that I'm

1202
00:52:23,810 --> 00:52:25,060
showing you here.

1203
00:52:25,060 --> 00:52:29,260
So here's an architecture for how to map from an image

1204
00:52:29,260 --> 00:52:30,245
to a sentence.

1205
00:52:30,245 --> 00:52:31,620
Sometimes, this problem is called

1206
00:52:31,620 --> 00:52:34,100
image captioning if I'm trying to take an image

1207
00:52:34,100 --> 00:52:36,412
and train the system to output the caption

1208
00:52:36,412 --> 00:52:38,620
that somebody would have written underneath the image

1209
00:52:38,620 --> 00:52:42,340
online, on websites, or maybe the alt text of the image.

1210
00:52:42,340 --> 00:52:46,420
So here's how to do that with a transformer.

1211
00:52:46,420 --> 00:52:50,280
I have my branch down here, which is the image encoder.

1212
00:52:50,280 --> 00:52:52,130
It will take an image.

1213
00:52:52,130 --> 00:52:54,380
And we talked about this in the transformers lecture--

1214
00:52:54,380 --> 00:52:57,220
that typically, we use these vision transformers, which

1215
00:52:57,220 --> 00:52:59,420
chop up the image into a set of patches,

1216
00:52:59,420 --> 00:53:01,580
embed each patch into a vector, and then just

1217
00:53:01,580 --> 00:53:05,640
run a transformer over those tokens

1218
00:53:05,640 --> 00:53:08,300
now and get an output set of tokens.

1219
00:53:08,300 --> 00:53:10,380
So now I've tokenized the input into

1220
00:53:10,380 --> 00:53:12,060
these nice, abstracted representations

1221
00:53:12,060 --> 00:53:14,740
of the-- of what's going on in that image.

1222
00:53:14,740 --> 00:53:19,740
So now this part here is just a GPT regular--

1223
00:53:19,740 --> 00:53:20,920
GPT is one name for it.

1224
00:53:20,920 --> 00:53:23,950
But it's just an autoregressive language model

1225
00:53:23,950 --> 00:53:25,630
with a transformer.

1226
00:53:25,630 --> 00:53:27,850
So what is that doing?

1227
00:53:27,850 --> 00:53:30,478
It's taking in the prefix to a sentence

1228
00:53:30,478 --> 00:53:32,270
and predicting the next word, or predicting

1229
00:53:32,270 --> 00:53:36,750
the next-- with causal attention, for every prefix,

1230
00:53:36,750 --> 00:53:38,470
I have a causal dependency between that

1231
00:53:38,470 --> 00:53:41,270
and the next token in the sequence.

1232
00:53:41,270 --> 00:53:45,470
So this word here, "sitting," only depends on all the previous

1233
00:53:45,470 --> 00:53:46,010
stuff.

1234
00:53:46,010 --> 00:53:47,370
"Bird" does not depend on "sitting."

1235
00:53:47,370 --> 00:53:48,530
It only depends on the previous stuff.

1236
00:53:48,530 --> 00:53:50,190
That's the causal attention trick.

1237
00:53:50,190 --> 00:53:53,110
And I just train with this next-token prediction

1238
00:53:53,110 --> 00:53:57,350
objective with self-attention, but causal masked.

1239
00:53:57,350 --> 00:53:59,670
Now, to stick them together, all I have to do is--

1240
00:53:59,670 --> 00:54:01,170
they're all just in token space now.

1241
00:54:01,170 --> 00:54:05,310
So all I have to do is prefix the set of language tokens

1242
00:54:05,310 --> 00:54:06,770
with the set of image tokens.

1243
00:54:06,770 --> 00:54:09,590
And now I will be basically reading off a--

1244
00:54:09,590 --> 00:54:12,108
given this token, this token, this token of vision,

1245
00:54:12,108 --> 00:54:14,650
what is going to be the next word after this word, this word,

1246
00:54:14,650 --> 00:54:15,830
this word?

1247
00:54:15,830 --> 00:54:18,870
And one of the very simple ways of stitching these networks

1248
00:54:18,870 --> 00:54:22,950
together is what's called cross-attention, which is just

1249
00:54:22,950 --> 00:54:27,390
to say, this GPT language model is

1250
00:54:27,390 --> 00:54:31,590
going to simply be prefixed with additional tokens that

1251
00:54:31,590 --> 00:54:38,370
can submit keys and values that are queried by these tokens.

1252
00:54:38,370 --> 00:54:42,690
So the query is emitted from the word "yellow."

1253
00:54:42,690 --> 00:54:45,590
That will then match the key for the bird because it's yellow.

1254
00:54:45,590 --> 00:54:47,390
Potentially, that would be a good strategy.

1255
00:54:47,390 --> 00:54:50,630
That will submit a value that gets integrated

1256
00:54:50,630 --> 00:54:53,990
into the token representation at this layer of the network, which

1257
00:54:53,990 --> 00:54:56,750
is now like a multi-modal representation of both

1258
00:54:56,750 --> 00:55:00,050
the words and the images.

1259
00:55:00,050 --> 00:55:04,950
So cross-attention is just attention, but going--

1260
00:55:04,950 --> 00:55:06,990
taking keys from-- and values from one

1261
00:55:06,990 --> 00:55:10,790
set and queries from a different set.

1262
00:55:10,790 --> 00:55:14,150
Any questions about that mechanism, a really easy way

1263
00:55:14,150 --> 00:55:17,310
to stitch things together?

1264
00:55:17,310 --> 00:55:17,990
Yeah?

1265
00:55:17,990 --> 00:55:22,240
AUDIENCE: Question on the text-- a yellow bird-- are they--

1266
00:55:22,240 --> 00:55:26,067
at what point are they provided?

1267
00:55:26,067 --> 00:55:28,400
PHILLIP ISOLA: So this would be, if I have training data

1268
00:55:28,400 --> 00:55:30,880
of the form x, y-- we're still in that setting--

1269
00:55:30,880 --> 00:55:33,520
the x is the image, and the y is the caption.

1270
00:55:33,520 --> 00:55:38,640
And I'm always going to be extracting tokens

1271
00:55:38,640 --> 00:55:39,800
from the image.

1272
00:55:39,800 --> 00:55:42,080
And then I will be autoregressively predicting

1273
00:55:42,080 --> 00:55:44,640
the next word, given the words I've so far predicted

1274
00:55:44,640 --> 00:55:46,200
in the caption.

1275
00:55:46,200 --> 00:55:47,880
Does that answer your question?

1276
00:55:47,880 --> 00:55:49,338
AUDIENCE: So at the very beginning,

1277
00:55:49,338 --> 00:55:50,380
there will be no text?

1278
00:55:50,380 --> 00:55:51,440
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1279
00:55:51,440 --> 00:55:55,400
This is, like, three steps of autoregression in.

1280
00:55:55,400 --> 00:55:58,160
But you train it all--

1281
00:55:58,160 --> 00:56:00,468
you-- at inference time, you do it sequentially.

1282
00:56:00,468 --> 00:56:02,260
But you do it in parallel at training time.

1283
00:56:02,260 --> 00:56:03,420
This is like training time.

1284
00:56:03,420 --> 00:56:04,800
Yeah, question?

1285
00:56:04,800 --> 00:56:09,000
AUDIENCE: At the cross-attention layer, if you use a--

1286
00:56:09,000 --> 00:56:13,720
say if you use the embeddings of the text as a query instead

1287
00:56:13,720 --> 00:56:16,890
of just the token-- so a learned representation-- would that

1288
00:56:16,890 --> 00:56:21,850
make the learning quicker, or it doesn't matter?

1289
00:56:21,850 --> 00:56:25,030
Instead of using the tokens of the text,

1290
00:56:25,030 --> 00:56:28,130
you use them and learn embedding another model,

1291
00:56:28,130 --> 00:56:30,930
and you use cross-attention to learn embeddings of the text

1292
00:56:30,930 --> 00:56:34,670
and learn the image versus just the tokens that were not used.

1293
00:56:34,670 --> 00:56:36,905
Does that accelerate the learning?

1294
00:56:36,905 --> 00:56:39,030
PHILLIP ISOLA: So I'm not sure I'm fully following.

1295
00:56:39,030 --> 00:56:40,488
Maybe we can talk about it offline.

1296
00:56:40,488 --> 00:56:42,530
But there's a lot of different strategies for how

1297
00:56:42,530 --> 00:56:45,330
to integrate this information.

1298
00:56:45,330 --> 00:56:48,290
Do I learn the--

1299
00:56:48,290 --> 00:56:50,610
typically, you have a query matrix

1300
00:56:50,610 --> 00:56:53,410
which applies to the text and another query matrix that

1301
00:56:53,410 --> 00:56:56,330
applies to the visual data.

1302
00:56:56,330 --> 00:56:59,770
And then you can submit keys, values, and queries

1303
00:56:59,770 --> 00:57:01,490
from both modalities.

1304
00:57:04,770 --> 00:57:10,410
So one of the trending industrial things this year

1305
00:57:10,410 --> 00:57:13,450
is multimodal chatbots.

1306
00:57:13,450 --> 00:57:15,902
So now all of the language models actually

1307
00:57:15,902 --> 00:57:17,110
are not just language models.

1308
00:57:17,110 --> 00:57:18,390
They take images as inputs.

1309
00:57:18,390 --> 00:57:20,410
Some of them even take videos as input.

1310
00:57:20,410 --> 00:57:21,690
This is ChatGPT.

1311
00:57:21,690 --> 00:57:22,770
And here's the slide.

1312
00:57:22,770 --> 00:57:23,868
I just take a screenshot.

1313
00:57:23,868 --> 00:57:25,410
I think some of you pointed out to me

1314
00:57:25,410 --> 00:57:27,077
that you can interpret our slides better

1315
00:57:27,077 --> 00:57:28,430
by just doing this.

1316
00:57:28,430 --> 00:57:30,410
And in fact, it actually works decently well.

1317
00:57:30,410 --> 00:57:32,910
So go ahead and do it if you want.

1318
00:57:32,910 --> 00:57:34,210
So what is this slide showing?

1319
00:57:34,210 --> 00:57:37,050
And how does it relate to how I work ChatGPT?

1320
00:57:37,050 --> 00:57:38,050
And it's pretty good.

1321
00:57:38,050 --> 00:57:41,010
You have an image encoder, cross-attention layer.

1322
00:57:41,010 --> 00:57:43,430
But it did get this wrong.

1323
00:57:43,430 --> 00:57:45,330
So how does it relate to how I work?

1324
00:57:45,330 --> 00:57:47,107
It says, well, I'm not a language--

1325
00:57:47,107 --> 00:57:48,190
I'm only a language model.

1326
00:57:48,190 --> 00:57:49,570
I don't process visual data.

1327
00:57:49,570 --> 00:57:52,790
And unless OpenAI is doing some crazy hackery--

1328
00:57:52,790 --> 00:57:54,770
it was looking at the visual data.

1329
00:57:54,770 --> 00:57:56,990
It had to be in order to make the correct assessment.

1330
00:57:56,990 --> 00:57:58,032
So I think this is wrong.

1331
00:57:58,032 --> 00:58:00,490
It doesn't know how it works.

1332
00:58:00,490 --> 00:58:02,790
It doesn't know how its own brain or itself works.

1333
00:58:05,193 --> 00:58:06,610
So let's go in the other direction

1334
00:58:06,610 --> 00:58:09,955
now and look at text-to-image.

1335
00:58:09,955 --> 00:58:11,330
And the interesting thing here is

1336
00:58:11,330 --> 00:58:14,940
that text-to-image models of the current generation, or maybe one

1337
00:58:14,940 --> 00:58:18,580
generation back, actually are a combination of GANs, VAEs,

1338
00:58:18,580 --> 00:58:20,420
and diffusion models.

1339
00:58:20,420 --> 00:58:21,740
They're all three.

1340
00:58:21,740 --> 00:58:24,128
So it's a nice-- people call them diffusion models.

1341
00:58:24,128 --> 00:58:25,920
But they have all three components in them.

1342
00:58:25,920 --> 00:58:28,100
So I'll tell you about that.

1343
00:58:28,100 --> 00:58:32,288
So here's the more general strategy

1344
00:58:32,288 --> 00:58:34,580
for translating between two different modalities or two

1345
00:58:34,580 --> 00:58:35,470
domains.

1346
00:58:35,470 --> 00:58:37,220
It's just a general conditional prediction

1347
00:58:37,220 --> 00:58:40,700
of one type of data from another type of data.

1348
00:58:40,700 --> 00:58:42,940
So now we have our data distribution.

1349
00:58:42,940 --> 00:58:45,680
That goes from encoder to this latent distribution.

1350
00:58:45,680 --> 00:58:49,540
And then if it's a-- if it's in the VAE autoencoder style,

1351
00:58:49,540 --> 00:58:51,940
then I decode from that latent space

1352
00:58:51,940 --> 00:58:54,900
now not back to in autoencoding, like back to x, but back

1353
00:58:54,900 --> 00:58:55,880
to another domain, y.

1354
00:58:55,880 --> 00:58:58,860
And I penalize the difference between my prediction and my--

1355
00:58:58,860 --> 00:59:00,960
the ground truth, y.

1356
00:59:00,960 --> 00:59:03,780
So this is just a general way to think about it.

1357
00:59:03,780 --> 00:59:06,800
So here's what that looks like in text-to-image,

1358
00:59:06,800 --> 00:59:08,120
which we talked about before.

1359
00:59:08,120 --> 00:59:14,820
So I go from image space to latent space to text space.

1360
00:59:14,820 --> 00:59:21,780
And that would map onto this, image space to latent space,

1361
00:59:21,780 --> 00:59:23,100
and then also text space.

1362
00:59:23,100 --> 00:59:24,500
Where do you define the latents is not clear

1363
00:59:24,500 --> 00:59:25,875
because it's autoregressive here.

1364
00:59:25,875 --> 00:59:27,500
That's not explicit latents.

1365
00:59:27,500 --> 00:59:29,940
But this would be more like the VAE way of doing it.

1366
00:59:29,940 --> 00:59:32,600
But now let's look at the text-to-image problem.

1367
00:59:32,600 --> 00:59:34,940
So go from text, "A yellow bird sitting on a branch,"

1368
00:59:34,940 --> 00:59:36,780
to the image.

1369
00:59:36,780 --> 00:59:40,220
And this one is going to follow this diagram a little bit more

1370
00:59:40,220 --> 00:59:42,580
precisely.

1371
00:59:42,580 --> 00:59:45,640
So I already told you about how to train a conditional VAE.

1372
00:59:45,640 --> 00:59:47,740
So how do you train a conditional VAE

1373
00:59:47,740 --> 00:59:49,740
when you're conditioning on text?

1374
00:59:49,740 --> 00:59:55,340
So rather than conditioning on the observation of that moving

1375
00:59:55,340 --> 00:59:57,260
pink block, I'm going to condition

1376
00:59:57,260 --> 01:00:01,020
on a scarlet macaw sitting on a perch.

1377
01:00:01,020 --> 01:00:03,100
So what's going to happen is the decoder

1378
01:00:03,100 --> 01:00:05,500
is going to be informed by the observation

1379
01:00:05,500 --> 01:00:07,200
as well as my latent variable, z,

1380
01:00:07,200 --> 01:00:09,790
that explains everything-- that has to explain everything

1381
01:00:09,790 --> 01:00:12,910
about the data to make this a deterministic process.

1382
01:00:12,910 --> 01:00:14,290
So the decoder is deterministic.

1383
01:00:14,290 --> 01:00:18,350
So this latent variable has to model all the random factors

1384
01:00:18,350 --> 01:00:23,110
that could affect the output appearance given the input.

1385
01:00:23,110 --> 01:00:25,830
So this input does not say that the scarlet macaw

1386
01:00:25,830 --> 01:00:27,535
is facing to the left.

1387
01:00:27,535 --> 01:00:28,910
So therefore, the latent variable

1388
01:00:28,910 --> 01:00:31,810
has to learn to encode that the macaw is facing to the left.

1389
01:00:31,810 --> 01:00:36,150
The latent variable is trained by inferring the latent variable

1390
01:00:36,150 --> 01:00:38,570
from the target prediction.

1391
01:00:38,570 --> 01:00:41,310
So this is like a VAE, and then conditioning.

1392
01:00:41,310 --> 01:00:43,270
It's a conditional VAE-- so same idea

1393
01:00:43,270 --> 01:00:46,030
that I showed on the previous slides.

1394
01:00:46,030 --> 01:00:47,950
And now the only new thing is I need

1395
01:00:47,950 --> 01:00:51,190
this to be a language model, f, that encodes text into tokens.

1396
01:00:51,190 --> 01:00:55,350
And I need this to be a model that could be a ViT.

1397
01:00:55,350 --> 01:00:57,550
A transformer decoder takes a set of tokens.

1398
01:00:57,550 --> 01:01:01,870
And the latent variables might also be tokens.

1399
01:01:01,870 --> 01:01:04,890
And at inference time, I simply sample from the unit Gaussian

1400
01:01:04,890 --> 01:01:07,920
because the VAE is trained to make the latent distribution

1401
01:01:07,920 --> 01:01:09,620
dense and compact.

1402
01:01:09,620 --> 01:01:13,000
And therefore, samples from the Gaussian are going to all map

1403
01:01:13,000 --> 01:01:16,680
to valid predictions that are all in distribution

1404
01:01:16,680 --> 01:01:19,920
with respect to how the model was trained.

1405
01:01:19,920 --> 01:01:21,320
Yeah, question?

1406
01:01:21,320 --> 01:01:24,680
AUDIENCE: If you go back to the previous slide, what's

1407
01:01:24,680 --> 01:01:28,620
the optimal strategy to incorporate this information?

1408
01:01:28,620 --> 01:01:32,640
Is it just, for example, to sum the two embedding vectors

1409
01:01:32,640 --> 01:01:35,000
or, for example, to concatenate and then

1410
01:01:35,000 --> 01:01:37,840
process through an MLP to have something

1411
01:01:37,840 --> 01:01:40,060
in low-dimension space?

1412
01:01:40,060 --> 01:01:42,560
PHILLIP ISOLA: The question is, what is the optimal strategy

1413
01:01:42,560 --> 01:01:44,440
for how to combine this information

1414
01:01:44,440 --> 01:01:46,000
with this information, I believe?

1415
01:01:46,000 --> 01:01:49,520
And I would say the vanilla, most standard thing right now

1416
01:01:49,520 --> 01:01:51,720
seems to be concatenation, like convert everything

1417
01:01:51,720 --> 01:01:54,480
into a set of tokens or a set of embedding vectors,

1418
01:01:54,480 --> 01:01:57,760
and then just concatenate and do more cross-attention

1419
01:01:57,760 --> 01:01:59,540
between them, and then repeat.

1420
01:01:59,540 --> 01:02:04,400
So what I showed on this slide is the current standard way

1421
01:02:04,400 --> 01:02:07,475
of combining between different streams of data.

1422
01:02:07,475 --> 01:02:08,600
Now, it's not the only way.

1423
01:02:08,600 --> 01:02:11,400
You could also sum them together, take an average,

1424
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:11,900
multiply.

1425
01:02:11,900 --> 01:02:13,233
There's all kinds of other ways.

1426
01:02:13,233 --> 01:02:15,420
But I would say this is the current one.

1427
01:02:18,920 --> 01:02:20,600
And how do you combine different streams

1428
01:02:20,600 --> 01:02:22,580
in a transformer in a more intelligent way?

1429
01:02:22,580 --> 01:02:24,100
That is an active area of research--

1430
01:02:24,100 --> 01:02:26,308
so maybe a good final project, compare different ways

1431
01:02:26,308 --> 01:02:29,040
of combining these things.

1432
01:02:29,040 --> 01:02:35,160
So that's how a conditional VAE can solve language-to-image.

1433
01:02:35,160 --> 01:02:38,340
And this is roughly the way that the Dall-E 1 model works.

1434
01:02:38,340 --> 01:02:43,200
So the Dall-E 1 was probably the first text-to-image model

1435
01:02:43,200 --> 01:02:46,120
that made a splash.

1436
01:02:46,120 --> 01:02:49,240
But then Stable Diffusion, Dall-E 2,

1437
01:02:49,240 --> 01:02:52,160
Midjourney, all these other things came out.

1438
01:02:52,160 --> 01:02:55,060
And they switched to using a diffusion model.

1439
01:02:55,060 --> 01:03:00,417
So how do I make a diffusion model do text/image generation?

1440
01:03:00,417 --> 01:03:02,000
So it turns out with diffusion models,

1441
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:03,640
it's really, really simple.

1442
01:03:03,640 --> 01:03:05,070
So here's my diffusion model.

1443
01:03:05,070 --> 01:03:07,810
I start with random noise, unit Gaussian noise.

1444
01:03:07,810 --> 01:03:09,890
I try to predict step by step.

1445
01:03:09,890 --> 01:03:13,570
And I've supervised it with the opposite direction.

1446
01:03:13,570 --> 01:03:14,570
My data point is here.

1447
01:03:14,570 --> 01:03:15,250
I add noise.

1448
01:03:15,250 --> 01:03:17,850
And that's my supervision for how to denoise.

1449
01:03:17,850 --> 01:03:20,950
I try to undo the noise I added.

1450
01:03:20,950 --> 01:03:23,370
So each step is a supervised learning problem

1451
01:03:23,370 --> 01:03:26,970
of, here's the target, the less noisy-- here's

1452
01:03:26,970 --> 01:03:28,390
my generated noisy image.

1453
01:03:28,390 --> 01:03:29,850
I try to invert that by predicting

1454
01:03:29,850 --> 01:03:31,790
what the less noisy image would look like,

1455
01:03:31,790 --> 01:03:33,410
given the more noisy image.

1456
01:03:33,410 --> 01:03:36,650
And to make it conditional on text, all I have to do

1457
01:03:36,650 --> 01:03:40,290
is let my denoiser observe the text.

1458
01:03:40,290 --> 01:03:41,228
Why does that work?

1459
01:03:41,228 --> 01:03:42,770
That works for the following reason--

1460
01:03:42,770 --> 01:03:47,770
because the text will be informative toward what

1461
01:03:47,770 --> 01:03:51,410
the true image I started with is.

1462
01:03:51,410 --> 01:03:54,755
So if the text says "A robin on a yellow background," well,

1463
01:03:54,755 --> 01:03:55,630
here's a noisy image.

1464
01:03:55,630 --> 01:03:56,070
I don't know.

1465
01:03:56,070 --> 01:03:57,050
It looks like it might be a little bit

1466
01:03:57,050 --> 01:03:58,592
of a yellow background, or maybe it's

1467
01:03:58,592 --> 01:04:00,370
a white background or a pink background.

1468
01:04:00,370 --> 01:04:06,132
The text disambiguates that if the text is a--

1469
01:04:06,132 --> 01:04:08,700
is actually-- if there is a dependency in the training

1470
01:04:08,700 --> 01:04:12,140
data between the text and the image, which there should be.

1471
01:04:12,140 --> 01:04:14,800
So to the degree to which there is that dependency,

1472
01:04:14,800 --> 01:04:17,240
the text will help you make a more correct prediction.

1473
01:04:17,240 --> 01:04:19,380
Therefore, f will learn to use the text

1474
01:04:19,380 --> 01:04:22,580
and learn to denoise in the direction guided by the text.

1475
01:04:22,580 --> 01:04:24,920
If the text were "A robin on a pink background,"

1476
01:04:24,920 --> 01:04:27,170
that would be a hint as to what the correct answer is.

1477
01:04:27,170 --> 01:04:30,180
And therefore, my denoiser would head more toward pink.

1478
01:04:30,180 --> 01:04:33,260
So the text will guide how the denoiser

1479
01:04:33,260 --> 01:04:37,460
maps to different attributes of the data to the degree

1480
01:04:37,460 --> 01:04:41,080
to which the text actually does describe the image content.

1481
01:04:44,365 --> 01:04:45,240
Does that make sense?

1482
01:04:45,240 --> 01:04:47,180
So it's really easy to make a diffusion model

1483
01:04:47,180 --> 01:04:49,140
into a conditional diffusion model.

1484
01:04:49,140 --> 01:04:52,460
Just let the denoiser take text as input.

1485
01:04:52,460 --> 01:04:55,240
It's really easy to make a GAN into a conditional GAN.

1486
01:04:55,240 --> 01:04:57,680
Just let the discriminator take the text as the input.

1487
01:04:57,680 --> 01:04:59,740
It's really easy to make an autoregressive model

1488
01:04:59,740 --> 01:05:00,940
into a conditional model.

1489
01:05:00,940 --> 01:05:02,420
It already is a conditional model.

1490
01:05:02,420 --> 01:05:04,760
The VAE was the only one that was a little bit tricky.

1491
01:05:09,100 --> 01:05:11,513
Here's how Stable Diffusion from a few years ago worked.

1492
01:05:11,513 --> 01:05:13,680
Stable Diffusion has five or six different versions.

1493
01:05:13,680 --> 01:05:15,263
So I don't know which version this is.

1494
01:05:15,263 --> 01:05:17,780
But this was one of the versions.

1495
01:05:17,780 --> 01:05:20,680
So what it did is at every step of denoising,

1496
01:05:20,680 --> 01:05:23,400
the denoising network was a neural net called a U-Net.

1497
01:05:23,400 --> 01:05:26,700
It's just like a ConvNet with these skip connections.

1498
01:05:26,700 --> 01:05:28,680
So it was a convolutional architecture.

1499
01:05:28,680 --> 01:05:31,580
Now people are-- use what's called diffusion transformers,

1500
01:05:31,580 --> 01:05:32,720
where this is a ViT.

1501
01:05:32,720 --> 01:05:35,120
This is a transformer model.

1502
01:05:35,120 --> 01:05:38,300
But back a few years ago, this was a-- called a U-Net.

1503
01:05:38,300 --> 01:05:41,820
And the text information comes into the network

1504
01:05:41,820 --> 01:05:43,600
now not by just concatenating tokens,

1505
01:05:43,600 --> 01:05:45,558
because this was not like a token architecture.

1506
01:05:45,558 --> 01:05:48,020
This was a architecture before transformers.

1507
01:05:48,020 --> 01:05:49,660
And so you actually took the vectors

1508
01:05:49,660 --> 01:05:51,460
coming out of the text encoder.

1509
01:05:51,460 --> 01:05:55,540
And I think you modulated the batch

1510
01:05:55,540 --> 01:05:56,920
normalization or something.

1511
01:05:56,920 --> 01:05:59,030
There's ways of integrating--

1512
01:05:59,030 --> 01:06:01,030
scaling each of the activations by,

1513
01:06:01,030 --> 01:06:03,835
somehow, the outcoming vectors here.

1514
01:06:03,835 --> 01:06:05,210
So you could look up the details.

1515
01:06:05,210 --> 01:06:08,003
But I don't know if it matters that much.

1516
01:06:08,003 --> 01:06:10,670
But there's a lot of interesting ways of integrating information

1517
01:06:10,670 --> 01:06:13,290
from one data stream to another data stream.

1518
01:06:17,150 --> 01:06:19,870
And of course, once you have this ability to plug together

1519
01:06:19,870 --> 01:06:23,310
different modalities and solve general conditional prediction

1520
01:06:23,310 --> 01:06:27,350
problems, it's-- this is a very general thing.

1521
01:06:27,350 --> 01:06:29,870
I think that's clear from ChatGPT and language

1522
01:06:29,870 --> 01:06:33,390
models, where you can use them for so many purposes.

1523
01:06:33,390 --> 01:06:35,390
But here's just an example for what's

1524
01:06:35,390 --> 01:06:37,630
called the Visual Question Answering problem, where

1525
01:06:37,630 --> 01:06:40,680
I have a text encoder that takes a question as input,

1526
01:06:40,680 --> 01:06:41,930
turns it into a set of tokens.

1527
01:06:41,930 --> 01:06:42,870
I have an image encoder.

1528
01:06:42,870 --> 01:06:44,810
That turns it into a set of tokens across a tent.

1529
01:06:44,810 --> 01:06:46,560
And I have a decoder that tries to predict

1530
01:06:46,560 --> 01:06:48,050
the answer to the question.

1531
01:06:48,050 --> 01:06:51,230
And if this is trained on tuples of the form question, image,

1532
01:06:51,230 --> 01:06:54,390
answer, then it will learn to maximize

1533
01:06:54,390 --> 01:06:56,670
the conditional probability of the answer,

1534
01:06:56,670 --> 01:06:59,100
given the question and the image.

1535
01:06:59,100 --> 01:07:02,420
And that's general-purpose image understanding.

1536
01:07:02,420 --> 01:07:07,120
Whatever question you want to ask can be framed in this way.

1537
01:07:07,120 --> 01:07:11,400
So this is a very general-purpose technology.

1538
01:07:11,400 --> 01:07:13,170
And just to show you that I'm not--

1539
01:07:13,170 --> 01:07:15,420
I don't think I'm hiding too much under the hood here,

1540
01:07:15,420 --> 01:07:20,980
this is one of the current popular image-to-text--

1541
01:07:20,980 --> 01:07:25,258
or this is one of the current popular VQA models-- so image

1542
01:07:25,258 --> 01:07:27,300
question answering, or visual question answering.

1543
01:07:27,300 --> 01:07:28,720
It's called LLaVa.

1544
01:07:28,720 --> 01:07:31,940
So here's my image encoder, a set of tokens.

1545
01:07:31,940 --> 01:07:34,342
Here's my question encoder, or they

1546
01:07:34,342 --> 01:07:36,800
call it instruction because it could be an instruction, not

1547
01:07:36,800 --> 01:07:38,320
just a question.

1548
01:07:38,320 --> 01:07:39,520
That's a set of tokens.

1549
01:07:39,520 --> 01:07:40,460
They cross-attend.

1550
01:07:40,460 --> 01:07:42,480
And they out-- threw a transformer, a GPT,

1551
01:07:42,480 --> 01:07:44,800
and the output of language response.

1552
01:07:44,800 --> 01:07:45,780
That's pretty simple.

1553
01:07:50,420 --> 01:07:53,700
And I said that in the text-to-image direction,

1554
01:07:53,700 --> 01:07:55,520
actually, you're combining a lot of tricks.

1555
01:07:55,520 --> 01:07:57,223
All these models are very related.

1556
01:07:57,223 --> 01:07:58,640
And often, the tricks are actually

1557
01:07:58,640 --> 01:08:00,760
being shuffled between them.

1558
01:08:00,760 --> 01:08:04,440
So this was one of the important text-to-image models

1559
01:08:04,440 --> 01:08:05,540
from a few years ago.

1560
01:08:05,540 --> 01:08:09,840
This is still the core of the Stable Diffusion line of work,

1561
01:08:09,840 --> 01:08:12,160
at least up until they might now be doing things

1562
01:08:12,160 --> 01:08:16,500
with these flow-based models-- but just up to 2023, let's say.

1563
01:08:16,500 --> 01:08:19,540
So you actually first trained what's called a VQGAN.

1564
01:08:19,540 --> 01:08:23,120
That's a VAE with a patch GAN discriminator

1565
01:08:23,120 --> 01:08:25,143
that tries to make the output look sharp.

1566
01:08:25,143 --> 01:08:26,060
So it's like, I take--

1567
01:08:26,060 --> 01:08:27,120
I encode.

1568
01:08:27,120 --> 01:08:27,962
I decode.

1569
01:08:27,962 --> 01:08:29,420
I put that through a discriminator.

1570
01:08:29,420 --> 01:08:32,240
So it's like a combination of a VAE and a GAN.

1571
01:08:32,240 --> 01:08:36,680
And then in the latent space, I have either a diffusion model,

1572
01:08:36,680 --> 01:08:38,600
or here it's actually an autoregressive model

1573
01:08:38,600 --> 01:08:40,200
over the latent variables.

1574
01:08:40,200 --> 01:08:42,720
So you can just combine all of these things together.

1575
01:08:42,720 --> 01:08:45,120
And that sometimes gives you better results.

1576
01:08:49,080 --> 01:08:49,660
Question?

1577
01:08:49,660 --> 01:08:50,453
Yeah?

1578
01:08:50,453 --> 01:08:52,120
AUDIENCE: How do you creates these GANs?

1579
01:08:52,120 --> 01:08:57,029
It sounds like a [INAUDIBLE] separately, all together,

1580
01:08:57,029 --> 01:08:57,870
or [INAUDIBLE]?

1581
01:08:57,870 --> 01:08:58,750
PHILLIP ISOLA: Great question.

1582
01:08:58,750 --> 01:09:00,189
How do you train such a system?

1583
01:09:00,189 --> 01:09:02,590
It looks complicated-- backprop work.

1584
01:09:02,590 --> 01:09:05,630
So you can potentially backprop through the whole system.

1585
01:09:05,630 --> 01:09:07,029
That's one approach.

1586
01:09:07,029 --> 01:09:09,529
You can also pre-train subcomponents separately

1587
01:09:09,529 --> 01:09:12,450
if you have data for-- if you have some data which

1588
01:09:12,450 --> 01:09:15,170
is text paired with images and other data

1589
01:09:15,170 --> 01:09:18,189
that's just raw, unlabeled images, raw images without text,

1590
01:09:18,189 --> 01:09:21,410
you might pre-train the image-only part of the system.

1591
01:09:21,410 --> 01:09:25,290
You might pre-train this VQGAN with just the images

1592
01:09:25,290 --> 01:09:31,170
and then backprop through a interface with a language model

1593
01:09:31,170 --> 01:09:32,310
only on the paired data.

1594
01:09:32,310 --> 01:09:34,090
So there's a lot of ways of doing this.

1595
01:09:34,090 --> 01:09:35,670
I'm not going to go into this detail.

1596
01:09:35,670 --> 01:09:37,689
But there is what's called vector quantization

1597
01:09:37,689 --> 01:09:39,930
in the latent space in this particular model, which

1598
01:09:39,930 --> 01:09:41,510
makes backprop hard.

1599
01:09:41,510 --> 01:09:44,310
And so you have to deal with this vector quantization.

1600
01:09:44,310 --> 01:09:47,250
But I think-- I won't go into that today.

1601
01:09:47,250 --> 01:09:48,649
Yeah, question?

1602
01:09:48,649 --> 01:09:51,330
AUDIENCE: We have seen example of generating

1603
01:09:51,330 --> 01:09:52,950
an image or a text.

1604
01:09:52,950 --> 01:09:55,050
But what if-- for example, on ChatGPT, we

1605
01:09:55,050 --> 01:09:58,890
have this interaction of, given a text, generate image and text.

1606
01:09:58,890 --> 01:10:01,310
Do they go through one model, or actually

1607
01:10:01,310 --> 01:10:04,902
it's an engineering trick that [INAUDIBLE] give you two models?

1608
01:10:04,902 --> 01:10:06,110
PHILLIP ISOLA: Good question.

1609
01:10:06,110 --> 01:10:09,930
So in modern chatbots, usually, they

1610
01:10:09,930 --> 01:10:12,653
take image and text as input and output image and text.

1611
01:10:12,653 --> 01:10:14,570
And now some of them take audio as input, too.

1612
01:10:14,570 --> 01:10:15,670
And they'll output audio.

1613
01:10:15,670 --> 01:10:18,128
Eventually, they'll take all modalities as input and output

1614
01:10:18,128 --> 01:10:21,503
all modalities that we can think of.

1615
01:10:21,503 --> 01:10:23,170
I don't know how they work because these

1616
01:10:23,170 --> 01:10:26,650
are all now companies that don't tell us how they work.

1617
01:10:26,650 --> 01:10:28,990
There's one-- Llama 3.2, I believe--

1618
01:10:28,990 --> 01:10:33,690
which takes at least images and text, and maybe audio as well.

1619
01:10:33,690 --> 01:10:35,990
And that does have a paper that describes the details.

1620
01:10:35,990 --> 01:10:39,210
So I would look at that if you're interested.

1621
01:10:39,210 --> 01:10:41,870
So I don't remember how that one integrates information.

1622
01:10:45,590 --> 01:10:50,580
So any more questions on conditional generative models

1623
01:10:50,580 --> 01:10:55,828
for learning from paired data x, y?

1624
01:10:55,828 --> 01:10:56,332
Yeah?

1625
01:10:56,332 --> 01:10:58,040
AUDIENCE: As you increase the modalities,

1626
01:10:58,040 --> 01:11:00,683
it seems that they probably get increasingly data-hungry.

1627
01:11:00,683 --> 01:11:02,600
PHILLIP ISOLA: As you increase the modalities,

1628
01:11:02,600 --> 01:11:04,280
you would get increasingly data-hungry?

1629
01:11:04,280 --> 01:11:05,488
AUDIENCE: It seems like that.

1630
01:11:05,488 --> 01:11:07,780
Is that a problem we're running into?

1631
01:11:07,780 --> 01:11:10,540
PHILLIP ISOLA: That's a really interesting question.

1632
01:11:10,540 --> 01:11:13,160
I think it can go in either direction, in some sense.

1633
01:11:13,160 --> 01:11:16,220
So as you get more modalities, maybe there's

1634
01:11:16,220 --> 01:11:18,100
a more complicated--

1635
01:11:18,100 --> 01:11:21,940
maybe the mapping from x to y--

1636
01:11:21,940 --> 01:11:27,660
if I'm trying to map from input image to both sound and text,

1637
01:11:27,660 --> 01:11:30,617
maybe that's a function that just is more wiggly,

1638
01:11:30,617 --> 01:11:32,200
is more complicated function to learn.

1639
01:11:32,200 --> 01:11:33,533
So I need more data to learn it.

1640
01:11:33,533 --> 01:11:34,820
That's one possibility.

1641
01:11:34,820 --> 01:11:36,220
But it's also possible that there

1642
01:11:36,220 --> 01:11:39,500
are-- there's shared information between modalities.

1643
01:11:39,500 --> 01:11:44,570
So if I have more than one modality, then they can--

1644
01:11:44,570 --> 01:11:47,212
a pure language model that is just trained on language

1645
01:11:47,212 --> 01:11:48,670
might be worse at language modeling

1646
01:11:48,670 --> 01:11:52,032
than a language model that's trained on language and videos

1647
01:11:52,032 --> 01:11:53,990
because there's shared information in the video

1648
01:11:53,990 --> 01:11:55,813
about language information.

1649
01:11:55,813 --> 01:11:57,230
So that's a sense in which you get

1650
01:11:57,230 --> 01:11:59,370
an advantage, a statistical advantage,

1651
01:11:59,370 --> 01:12:00,810
by modeling more modalities.

1652
01:12:04,170 --> 01:12:07,350
So in this last little section, I want to say, wait a second.

1653
01:12:07,350 --> 01:12:12,830
So far, all of this lecture is about P Y given X for arbitrary

1654
01:12:12,830 --> 01:12:16,163
high-dimensional X and Y. And I've said that you can--

1655
01:12:16,163 --> 01:12:18,330
but the thing that I haven't really explicitly said,

1656
01:12:18,330 --> 01:12:20,030
but has been the underlying assumption,

1657
01:12:20,030 --> 01:12:23,290
is that you have data that comes paired.

1658
01:12:23,290 --> 01:12:29,102
So my P data distribution is over x, y pairs.

1659
01:12:29,102 --> 01:12:31,310
And I can sample from that, get pairs, and then model

1660
01:12:31,310 --> 01:12:32,990
the conditionals.

1661
01:12:32,990 --> 01:12:39,110
So what happens if I actually just have data x and data y,

1662
01:12:39,110 --> 01:12:42,270
but I have no examples of x paired with y?

1663
01:12:42,270 --> 01:12:43,790
Can I do anything?

1664
01:12:43,790 --> 01:12:46,270
And it turns out you can.

1665
01:12:46,270 --> 01:12:48,910
And oftentimes, you can do conditional modeling

1666
01:12:48,910 --> 01:12:50,390
with unpaired data.

1667
01:12:50,390 --> 01:12:53,590
And this is sometimes called unpaired translation.

1668
01:12:53,590 --> 01:12:59,630
So can I translate this cartoon into this more nice Valentine?

1669
01:12:59,630 --> 01:13:05,030
This is from 1968, this cartoon.

1670
01:13:05,030 --> 01:13:06,510
So here's the setting.

1671
01:13:06,510 --> 01:13:08,950
So paired data for a colorization problem--

1672
01:13:08,950 --> 01:13:10,802
I have black and white and color.

1673
01:13:10,802 --> 01:13:12,010
That's really easy to obtain.

1674
01:13:12,010 --> 01:13:12,990
I just take a color image.

1675
01:13:12,990 --> 01:13:14,170
I remove the color channels.

1676
01:13:14,170 --> 01:13:15,087
I get black and white.

1677
01:13:15,087 --> 01:13:16,323
It's paired data.

1678
01:13:16,323 --> 01:13:17,990
I can model the conditional distribution

1679
01:13:17,990 --> 01:13:19,910
of color given black and white using

1680
01:13:19,910 --> 01:13:22,610
the techniques that we saw.

1681
01:13:22,610 --> 01:13:26,710
But what if I want to predict, what would Cézanne

1682
01:13:26,710 --> 01:13:29,590
have painted if he had been standing at this river?

1683
01:13:29,590 --> 01:13:31,670
I can't ask Cézanne to go and paint

1684
01:13:31,670 --> 01:13:32,850
a million different rivers.

1685
01:13:32,850 --> 01:13:34,172
And I don't even know.

1686
01:13:34,172 --> 01:13:35,630
There's no photograph of this house

1687
01:13:35,630 --> 01:13:38,030
that he was-- he painted here.

1688
01:13:38,030 --> 01:13:40,050
So I don't have paired data.

1689
01:13:40,050 --> 01:13:43,310
And this is a potentially more common situation

1690
01:13:43,310 --> 01:13:45,920
than the situations in which we have paired data.

1691
01:13:45,920 --> 01:13:49,182
Having paired data-- it's a big constraint.

1692
01:13:49,182 --> 01:13:51,640
Not only do I have to have both the x's and the y's, I also

1693
01:13:51,640 --> 01:13:52,920
have to have the mapping between them,

1694
01:13:52,920 --> 01:13:54,340
or the pairing between them.

1695
01:13:54,340 --> 01:13:56,820
Unpaired data-- it comes across all the time.

1696
01:13:56,820 --> 01:13:57,880
Here's a simple example.

1697
01:13:57,880 --> 01:13:59,880
I have a set of Cézanne photos-- a set

1698
01:13:59,880 --> 01:14:02,240
of Cézanne paintings, a set of photos.

1699
01:14:02,240 --> 01:14:05,117
And as a human, you can reason about the stylistic differences

1700
01:14:05,117 --> 01:14:05,700
between those.

1701
01:14:05,700 --> 01:14:07,825
And you can imagine what Cézanne would have painted

1702
01:14:07,825 --> 01:14:09,640
for each of these photos.

1703
01:14:09,640 --> 01:14:11,440
How did you do that, despite never having

1704
01:14:11,440 --> 01:14:16,080
seen a side-by-side photo of a place and what Cézanne painted?

1705
01:14:16,080 --> 01:14:19,280
This is the problem of unpaired learning.

1706
01:14:19,280 --> 01:14:22,380
And there's a number of different methods.

1707
01:14:22,380 --> 01:14:26,160
So I'm going to talk about one in a little detail called--

1708
01:14:26,160 --> 01:14:28,240
doing this with GANs.

1709
01:14:28,240 --> 01:14:30,828
So here's a really cool thing about GANs

1710
01:14:30,828 --> 01:14:33,120
and why they're still a neat model, even though they're

1711
01:14:33,120 --> 01:14:35,040
less flashy these days.

1712
01:14:35,040 --> 01:14:38,760
So with a GAN, the pairing between the input and the output

1713
01:14:38,760 --> 01:14:41,320
is only coming through the discriminator, which looks at,

1714
01:14:41,320 --> 01:14:43,130
does the output go with the input?

1715
01:14:43,130 --> 01:14:45,130
But I could drop that.

1716
01:14:45,130 --> 01:14:46,020
Oh, sorry.

1717
01:14:46,020 --> 01:14:47,270
The animation is a little off.

1718
01:14:47,270 --> 01:14:48,530
I could drop--

1719
01:14:48,530 --> 01:14:55,090
I could-- so let's see.

1720
01:14:55,090 --> 01:14:57,482
So the problem with not having paired data is I

1721
01:14:57,482 --> 01:14:59,690
don't know what the Cézanne painting of this location

1722
01:14:59,690 --> 01:15:00,690
would be.

1723
01:15:00,690 --> 01:15:04,737
But let me go back and just drop that dependency.

1724
01:15:04,737 --> 01:15:06,070
The animations are a little off.

1725
01:15:06,070 --> 01:15:08,930
But let me drop that dependency where the discriminator looks

1726
01:15:08,930 --> 01:15:12,410
at the pair and say, what if the discriminator actually

1727
01:15:12,410 --> 01:15:14,830
only looks at the output and asks,

1728
01:15:14,830 --> 01:15:17,290
does this look like a Cézanne painting or not?

1729
01:15:17,290 --> 01:15:20,970
Now this is the traditional unconditional GAN objective.

1730
01:15:20,970 --> 01:15:24,210
I'm just trying to look at the outputs, my synthetic imagery

1731
01:15:24,210 --> 01:15:26,890
generated from z or x.

1732
01:15:26,890 --> 01:15:30,007
And I'm just trying to say, does it look like a real Cézanne

1733
01:15:30,007 --> 01:15:32,090
painting, or does it look like a synthetic Cézanne

1734
01:15:32,090 --> 01:15:34,410
painting made by the generator?

1735
01:15:34,410 --> 01:15:36,510
And this is a cool property of the GAN loss.

1736
01:15:36,510 --> 01:15:39,210
It's actually just checking whether the output belongs

1737
01:15:39,210 --> 01:15:41,810
to the set of good answers, not checking

1738
01:15:41,810 --> 01:15:45,950
whether the output matches the input.

1739
01:15:45,950 --> 01:15:48,530
So it's suitable for unpaired learning.

1740
01:15:48,530 --> 01:15:51,210
But how do I actually make the output not just

1741
01:15:51,210 --> 01:15:56,170
any random Cézanne photo, but actually match the input?

1742
01:15:56,170 --> 01:15:57,930
Sorry.

1743
01:15:57,930 --> 01:16:00,530
So here is what looks like a Cézanne painting that

1744
01:16:00,530 --> 01:16:01,730
matches the input.

1745
01:16:01,730 --> 01:16:04,130
But here is a--

1746
01:16:04,130 --> 01:16:08,010
well, the animations are off here.

1747
01:16:08,010 --> 01:16:12,770
But here's also a perfectly valid Cézanne painting.

1748
01:16:12,770 --> 01:16:17,050
And the discriminator will say that's real, too.

1749
01:16:17,050 --> 01:16:19,850
So the trick that works in this case

1750
01:16:19,850 --> 01:16:23,290
is to use this idea of cycle consistency.

1751
01:16:23,290 --> 01:16:25,730
So I'm going to impose the constraint

1752
01:16:25,730 --> 01:16:29,290
that the mapping from X to Y is consistent

1753
01:16:29,290 --> 01:16:31,762
with a backwards mapping from Y to X.

1754
01:16:31,762 --> 01:16:33,970
So there's some kind of bijectivity between these two

1755
01:16:33,970 --> 01:16:34,870
domains.

1756
01:16:34,870 --> 01:16:38,210
So I'm going to say I'll train a network to map from photos

1757
01:16:38,210 --> 01:16:40,038
to Cézanne on paintings according

1758
01:16:40,038 --> 01:16:41,580
to a discriminator that says, does it

1759
01:16:41,580 --> 01:16:42,788
look like a Cézanne painting?

1760
01:16:42,788 --> 01:16:44,602
Did I get to that domain output?

1761
01:16:44,602 --> 01:16:46,060
And then I'll train a discriminator

1762
01:16:46,060 --> 01:16:48,520
in the opposite direction to go from Cézanne paintings

1763
01:16:48,520 --> 01:16:49,660
back to photos.

1764
01:16:49,660 --> 01:16:52,600
And that should fool a discriminator that will ask,

1765
01:16:52,600 --> 01:16:53,797
does it look like a photo?

1766
01:16:53,797 --> 01:16:55,380
So the discriminators will make sure I

1767
01:16:55,380 --> 01:16:57,460
map to the domain of Cézanne paintings

1768
01:16:57,460 --> 01:17:00,085
and the domain of photos.

1769
01:17:00,085 --> 01:17:01,460
But I'll train these things to be

1770
01:17:01,460 --> 01:17:03,460
what's called cycle-consistent, which just means

1771
01:17:03,460 --> 01:17:07,427
that if I go from domain X to domain Y and then I go back,

1772
01:17:07,427 --> 01:17:08,760
I should arrive where I started.

1773
01:17:08,760 --> 01:17:11,440
And if I go from domain Y to domain X and I go back,

1774
01:17:11,440 --> 01:17:14,380
I should arrive where I started.

1775
01:17:14,380 --> 01:17:16,300
So this is kind of like--

1776
01:17:16,300 --> 01:17:17,922
in language, people use this, too.

1777
01:17:17,922 --> 01:17:19,380
Actually, there's an old Mark Twain

1778
01:17:19,380 --> 01:17:23,100
story about trying to translate between a French speaker

1779
01:17:23,100 --> 01:17:29,300
and an English speaker, where you would translate

1780
01:17:29,300 --> 01:17:32,420
into French and then back translates into English

1781
01:17:32,420 --> 01:17:34,900
and check that-- and you know that the system

1782
01:17:34,900 --> 01:17:38,040
is consistent with itself if you arrive where you started.

1783
01:17:38,040 --> 01:17:40,110
If I say an English word, then I say,

1784
01:17:40,110 --> 01:17:41,860
this is the French word, and then somebody

1785
01:17:41,860 --> 01:17:43,740
that speaks French tries to translate that French word back

1786
01:17:43,740 --> 01:17:45,960
into English, if I arrive back where I started,

1787
01:17:45,960 --> 01:17:50,720
that's a property that should be satisfied by natural languages

1788
01:17:50,720 --> 01:17:53,420
because natural languages tend to have

1789
01:17:53,420 --> 01:17:56,920
a bijection between the different languages,

1790
01:17:56,920 --> 01:17:59,380
like every French word has a roughly

1791
01:17:59,380 --> 01:18:01,802
direct translation to an English word-- not perfectly.

1792
01:18:01,802 --> 01:18:03,260
So sorry, French speakers, if maybe

1793
01:18:03,260 --> 01:18:06,240
there's concepts you can only express in French-- but roughly.

1794
01:18:09,618 --> 01:18:11,160
So that actually works decently well.

1795
01:18:11,160 --> 01:18:13,700
And that leads to results that look

1796
01:18:13,700 --> 01:18:15,780
like this, where I've converted this photo

1797
01:18:15,780 --> 01:18:18,420
into a synthetic Cézanne painting

1798
01:18:18,420 --> 01:18:21,380
without having paired data.

1799
01:18:21,380 --> 01:18:26,100
So one question you might have is, wait a second.

1800
01:18:26,100 --> 01:18:28,220
There must be many cycle-consistent mappings

1801
01:18:28,220 --> 01:18:32,340
between any set of images x and any set of images y.

1802
01:18:32,340 --> 01:18:34,740
And indeed, that's true.

1803
01:18:34,740 --> 01:18:37,253
But there is some theory around this

1804
01:18:37,253 --> 01:18:38,670
that's been developed to say that,

1805
01:18:38,670 --> 01:18:43,070
well, under the constraints of a particular neural net

1806
01:18:43,070 --> 01:18:46,550
architecture in a particular optimizer,

1807
01:18:46,550 --> 01:18:50,030
only some of the possible mappings from inputs to outputs

1808
01:18:50,030 --> 01:18:51,870
are going to be found.

1809
01:18:51,870 --> 01:18:53,833
So as long as I have some constraints

1810
01:18:53,833 --> 01:18:55,750
that say that I need to find a simple mapping,

1811
01:18:55,750 --> 01:18:57,297
in a certain sense--

1812
01:18:57,297 --> 01:18:59,130
and neural networks do find simple mappings.

1813
01:18:59,130 --> 01:19:00,670
We've talked about that.

1814
01:19:00,670 --> 01:19:05,030
So here's a ReLU network with a two-dimensional hidden state.

1815
01:19:05,030 --> 01:19:07,488
It's trying to map this line segment A to this line segment

1816
01:19:07,488 --> 01:19:07,988
B.

1817
01:19:07,988 --> 01:19:09,650
And there's infinite possible mappings.

1818
01:19:09,650 --> 01:19:12,390
But a ReLU network trained with gradient descent

1819
01:19:12,390 --> 01:19:15,200
with two hidden units can only find two mappings in this case,

1820
01:19:15,200 --> 01:19:16,450
will converge on two mappings.

1821
01:19:16,450 --> 01:19:17,790
One is the identity mapping.

1822
01:19:17,790 --> 01:19:20,590
And the other is the flipping of the lines,

1823
01:19:20,590 --> 01:19:23,790
like negative identity.

1824
01:19:23,790 --> 01:19:25,440
So why is that?

1825
01:19:25,440 --> 01:19:27,190
There are also complicated wiggly mappings

1826
01:19:27,190 --> 01:19:29,950
where every single point on A maps to a different random point

1827
01:19:29,950 --> 01:19:31,830
on B. But it's very hard for those

1828
01:19:31,830 --> 01:19:34,120
to be found by the architectures that we use

1829
01:19:34,120 --> 01:19:35,800
and the optimizers that we use.

1830
01:19:35,800 --> 01:19:38,420
So under the constraint of having a simple mapping,

1831
01:19:38,420 --> 01:19:41,860
it will tend to be the case that all map

1832
01:19:41,860 --> 01:19:43,173
the green stuff to green stuff.

1833
01:19:43,173 --> 01:19:44,840
That will be an easier to learn mapping.

1834
01:19:44,840 --> 01:19:48,520
And that disambiguates that it should be green to green instead

1835
01:19:48,520 --> 01:19:49,540
of green to blue.

1836
01:19:52,500 --> 01:19:55,165
And these ideas show up also in unpaired language translation.

1837
01:19:55,165 --> 01:19:56,540
And potentially in other domains,

1838
01:19:56,540 --> 01:19:59,280
you could apply them, too, where we

1839
01:19:59,280 --> 01:20:01,920
try to impose what they call back translation or cycle

1840
01:20:01,920 --> 01:20:04,080
consistency between the mapping from one language

1841
01:20:04,080 --> 01:20:05,780
to the other and back.

1842
01:20:05,780 --> 01:20:07,280
And there's a number of other tricks

1843
01:20:07,280 --> 01:20:09,960
that you can apply, as well, in order

1844
01:20:09,960 --> 01:20:12,040
to solve unpaired translations.

1845
01:20:12,040 --> 01:20:14,540
So I'm not going to have time to go into those other tricks.

1846
01:20:14,540 --> 01:20:17,360
But the general idea is if I have a distribution of data

1847
01:20:17,360 --> 01:20:20,560
in one modality indicated-- this is like the data distribution

1848
01:20:20,560 --> 01:20:22,340
of the red modality.

1849
01:20:22,340 --> 01:20:24,480
This is the data distribution of the blue modality.

1850
01:20:24,480 --> 01:20:25,980
Well, I can just align their shapes.

1851
01:20:25,980 --> 01:20:28,160
It's like a rigid transformation problem.

1852
01:20:28,160 --> 01:20:32,120
So under this idea of distributional--

1853
01:20:32,120 --> 01:20:37,840
the two distributions should somehow be the same.

1854
01:20:37,840 --> 01:20:39,520
And I can align those two distributions,

1855
01:20:39,520 --> 01:20:42,368
or they should be the same up to an isometric transformation.

1856
01:20:42,368 --> 01:20:44,160
So if you're interested in other approaches

1857
01:20:44,160 --> 01:20:47,040
to unpaired translation, look at this paper and look

1858
01:20:47,040 --> 01:20:48,760
at distributional alignment ideas

1859
01:20:48,760 --> 01:20:51,960
from natural language processing.

1860
01:20:51,960 --> 01:20:54,760
So the point I'll end on is this concept

1861
01:20:54,760 --> 01:20:56,720
that you don't always need paired data

1862
01:20:56,720 --> 01:21:00,200
to solved conditional prediction.

1863
01:21:00,200 --> 01:21:03,360
And one name for pairing is grounding, grounding

1864
01:21:03,360 --> 01:21:04,720
one modality in another.

1865
01:21:04,720 --> 01:21:06,762
And there's just really interesting implications,

1866
01:21:06,762 --> 01:21:10,080
like I could potentially identify the meaning of a word

1867
01:21:10,080 --> 01:21:12,400
without ever having seen a paired example of what

1868
01:21:12,400 --> 01:21:14,520
that word corresponds to in the physical world.

1869
01:21:14,520 --> 01:21:17,920
And I'll have more to say on that in some future lectures.

1870
01:21:17,920 --> 01:21:19,750
Thank you.
